{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018ix8cSZArxU8LgGwm2z2hn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# 05 -- Review context, repository navigation tools, grounding\n2\t\n3\t## 1. Purpose\n4\t\n5\tThis area decides what repository content the **finder** and **verifier**\n6\tcan see during one review, and checks afterwards that every finding quotes\n7\tcode the finder could actually have seen.\n8\t\n9\tIt has four parts:\n10\t\n11\t1. **The reviewed tree** (`Tree`). One review reads one corpus. A staged\n12\t   review reads an immutable tree written from the index. A normal review\n13\t   reads the immutable \"captured worktree\" tree (tracked files plus\n14\t   untracked, non-ignored, non-denylisted files) that the snapshot area\n15\t   writes. If no tree can be pinned, readers fall back to the live working\n16\t   tree and enforce the same corpus rules on every read. Every reader in\n17\t   this area (file context, the three exploration tools, the grounder)\n18\t   takes the same `Tree`, so later checkout or index edits cannot change\n19\t   what a running review sees.\n20\t2. **File context** for the prompts: numbered, middle-truncated snippets\n21\t   of the reviewed files for the finder's user prompt, and one snippet of\n22\t   the cited file for each verifier prompt.\n23\t3. **Exploration tools** handed to the agent loop: `read_file`,\n24\t   `list_files`, `grep_repo` (finder in interactive mode, verifier\n25\t   always). The Go baseline's `git_diff` tool and the grounder's range\n26\t   corpus are dead code there and are intentionally absent from the TS\n27\t   port (deliberate change 23 in `spec.md`).\n28\t4. **Quote grounding**: the check `review.Validate` uses to drop a\n29\t   finding whose `code_quote` does not appear (modulo whitespace) in the\n30\t   cited file's content or the cited file's section of the reviewed diff.\n31\t\n32\tAll model-supplied paths and patterns are untrusted. Repository content is\n33\tattacker-controlled. Every tool result is size-bounded and every path is\n34\tconfined to the reviewed corpus; nothing in this area ever serves `.git`\n35\tcontent, ignored files, or untracked denylisted credentials.\n36\t\n37\tThe agent loop that calls the tools, their per-call timeout, and the JSON\n38\tencoding of results belong to the LLM area. The scope area owns the diff,\n39\tthe file list, the manifest, `PatchFlags`, and fitting. The snapshot area\n40\towns `PinWorktree` and the secret denylist. The prompts area renders the\n41\tuser prompts that embed this area's text.\n42\t\n43\t## 2. Behavior contract\n44\t\n45\t### 2.1 Constants and limits\n46\t\n47\tAll byte counts are UTF-8 bytes, never UTF-16 code units.\n48\t\n49\t| Name | Value | Meaning |\n50\t| --- | --- | --- |\n51\t| `TOOL_OUTPUT_CAP` | 65536 | Ceiling on one tool result's content |\n52\t| `MAX_LINE_BYTES` | 32768 | Ceiling on one served `read_file` line |\n53\t| `READ_FILE_LINE_LIMIT` | 2000 | Max lines in one `read_file` window |\n54\t| `GREP_LINE_BYTES` | 2048 | Ceiling on one `grep_repo` match line |\n55\t| `LIST_FILES_CAP` | 500 | Max entries in one `list_files` result |\n56\t| `GREP_MATCH_CAP` | 200 | Max match lines in one `grep_repo` result |\n57\t| `GROUNDING_CACHE_BYTES` | 8388608 | Max file normalized whole |\n58\t| `MAX_WINDOW` | 1073741824 | Clamp on parsed `offset` / `limit` (2^30) |\n59\t| `LIST_PATTERN_MAX` | 256 | Max `list_files` pattern length in bytes |\n60\t| default window | offset 1, limit 500 | `parseOffsetLimit` defaults |\n61\t| stream buffer | 65536 | Read buffer for streamed git output |\n62\t\n63\tValues used by callers (CLI area) and restated here so the area is\n64\tcomplete:\n65\t\n66\t- Finder file context: `BuildFileContext(repo, files, budget, tree)` with\n67\t  `budget` = `limits.max_file_ctx_bytes` from the project config, default\n68\t  400000, or 100000 under `--quick`. Each file's snippet is rendered with\n69\t  `maxBytes` 120000.\n70\t- Verifier snippet: `NumberedSnippet(repo, finding.file, 60000, tree)`.\n71\t\n72\t### 2.2 The reviewed tree\n73\t\n74\t```ts\n75\ttype Tree = {\n76\t    oid:string        // '' means the live working tree\n77\t    worktree:boolean  // true: captured worktree; false + oid: index tree\n78\t}\n79\t```\n80\t\n81\t- `pinned(t)` is `t.oid !== ''`.\n82\t- `staged(t)` is `pinned(t) && !t.worktree` (the index corpus).\n83\t- `pinnedWorktree(oid)` returns `{oid, worktree: oid !== ''}`.\n84\t- The zero tree `{oid: '', worktree: false}` is the live working tree.\n85\t\n86\tThe CLI chooses the tree once per review:\n87\t\n88\t1. `--staged`: `pinIndexTree(repo)` before building scope. Its failure is\n89\t   fatal; the CLI wraps the message as `pin staged tree: <err>`.\n90\t2. Otherwise, after building scope, `snapshot.pinWorktree(repo)`. On\n91\t   success the tree is `pinnedWorktree(pin.tree)`. On failure the CLI\n92\t   logs `could not pin the working tree (<err>); reading it live with\n93\t   .git, ignored, and denylisted paths blocked` and uses the live tree.\n94\t\n95\t#### `pinIndexTree(repo)`\n96\t\n97\tWrites the current index into an immutable tree object without touching\n98\tthe live index, any ref, or any commit.\n99\t\n100\t1. `git -C <repo> rev-parse --absolute-git-dir` -> `gitDir` (stdout\n101\t   trimmed). Failure -> error `resolve git dir: <err>`.\n102\t2. The live index path is `$GIT_INDEX_FILE` when set (joined onto `repo`\n103\t   when relative), else `<gitDir>/index`. Honoring `GIT_INDEX_FILE` is\n104\t   required: inside the pre-commit gate Git points it at a temporary index\n105\t   holding exactly the commit's content.\n106\t3. Create a temp directory with prefix `roast-pin-` under the OS temp dir;\n107\t   failure -> `temp dir: <err>`. It is removed on every exit path.\n108\t4. Copy the live index to `<tmp>/index` with mode `0600`. A missing live\n109\t   index is not an error (Git treats it as an empty index): skip the copy.\n110\t   Errors: `read <src>: <err>`, `create <dst>: <err>`, `copy index: <err>`.\n111\t5. `git -C <repo> write-tree` with `GIT_INDEX_FILE=<tmp>/index` added to\n112\t   the inherited environment. Failure -> `write-tree: <err>`. An index with\n113\t   unmerged entries fails here.\n114\t6. Return `{oid: <trimmed stdout>, worktree: false}`.\n115\t\n116\tGit failures in this helper fold stderr into the error:\n117\t`<exec error>: <trimmed stderr>` when stderr is non-empty.\n118\t\n119\t### 2.3 Path confinement\n120\t\n121\t#### `resolveUnderRepo(repo, userPath)` -> absolute path or reject\n122\t\n123\tThe single chokepoint for model-supplied paths on the live tree.\n124\t\n125\t1. Reject `''` and absolute paths.\n126\t2. `root` = realpath of `repo` (fall back to `repo` on error).\n127\t3. `candidate` = `root` joined with the Go-`Clean`ed `userPath` (lexical:\n128\t   collapse `.`/`..`/duplicate slashes, drop a trailing slash).\n129\t4. `resolved` = `resolveDeepest(candidate)`: realpath of `candidate`; if\n130\t   that fails, realpath of its parent, and so on up to `/`. The\n131\t   non-existent tail is dropped, not re-appended.\n132\t5. Reject unless `resolved === root` or `resolved` starts with\n133\t   `root + '/'`.\n134\t6. Reject if `inGitDir(root, candidate)` or `inGitDir(root, resolved)`:\n135\t   the path relative to `root` has any segment equal to `.git`\n136\t   case-insensitively (so `.GIT/config` is refused on case-insensitive\n137\t   file systems). A relative-path computation error also rejects.\n138\t7. Return `candidate` (not `resolved`).\n139\t\n140\tExamples: `''`, `/etc/passwd`, `../escape`, `sub/../../etc/passwd`, and\n141\t`escape_link/secret.txt` (symlink to a directory outside the repo) are\n142\trejected; `internal/foo.go` and `.` are accepted.\n143\t\n144\t#### `resolveLive(repo, userPath)`\n145\t\n146\t`resolveUnderRepo`, then the corpus rules a pinned tree enforces by\n147\tconstruction, applied to the path as given and, when different, to its\n148\tsymlink target (`resolveDeepest(candidate)`), each taken relative to\n149\t`root`:\n150\t\n151\t- `rel === '.'` is allowed.\n152\t- If `rel` matches the secret denylist (snapshot area, `isDenylisted`) and\n153\t  is not tracked, reject. Tracked means\n154\t  `git -C <repo> ls-files -z --cached -- <rel>` (with\n155\t  `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1`) exits 0 with non-empty stdout.\n156\t- `git -C <repo> check-ignore -q -- ./<rel>` with\n157\t  `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0` in the environment (check-ignore refuses\n158\t  literal mode; the `./` prefix stops a leading `:` from being read as\n159\t  pathspec magic). Allowed only when it exits with code 1 (\"not ignored\").\n160\t  Exit 0 (ignored) or any other failure rejects (fail closed).\n161\t\n162\tExample: `gitlink -> .git` symlink: `gitlink/config` is rejected because\n163\tits target is inside `.git`.\n164\t\n165\t#### `stagedPathIsMagic(relpath)` / `stagedPathCanonical(relpath)`\n166\t\n167\tPinned-tree reads spell blobs as `<oid>:<relpath>`, so a model-supplied\n168\tpath could land in Git's revision grammar. `stagedPathIsMagic` returns\n169\ttrue (reject) when any of:\n170\t\n171\t- `relpath === ''` or starts with `:`;\n172\t- `relpath[1] === ':'` and `relpath[0]` is `0`..`3` (merge stage selector,\n173\t  e.g. `0:foo.go`);\n174\t- splitting on `/` yields any segment that is `''`, `.` or `..` (covers\n175\t  `/foo.go`, `./foo.go`, `../foo.go`, `sub/../foo.go`, `sub//foo.go`,\n176\t  `foo.go/.`, and a trailing slash).\n177\t\n178\t`stagedPathCanonical` is its negation. A pinned-tree reader never runs\n179\t`resolveUnderRepo`; canonical spelling is its only path check, and the\n180\tpinned tree contains no `.git`, ignored, or denylisted entries.\n181\t\n182\t#### Git invocation rules\n183\t\n184\t- Every git call is `git -C <repo> ...` via `execFile` (no shell), with\n185\t  the inherited environment plus any listed overrides.\n186\t- \"literal git\" means the environment gains `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1`.\n187\t- Model-supplied paths always follow `--`.\n188\t- Commands whose output may be large are streamed and the child process is\n189\t  killed (not drained) once the reader has what it needs.\n190\t- Every git child process this area spawns is also killed when the\n191\t  caller's `AbortSignal` fires (`spec.md` cross-cutting \"Process\n192\t  lifetime\"), including blocking calls such as `cat-file -s`,\n193\t  `check-ignore`, `ls-files`, and `write-tree`.\n194\t- Git output is read as `Buffer`. Bytes are decoded to a string with\n195\t  U+FFFD replacement only where the text goes to a model (tool results,\n196\t  prompt snippets). Hashing and matching paths stay on bytes (`spec.md`\n197\t  cross-cutting \"Bytes and text\").\n198\t\n199\t### 2.4 Blob reads from a pinned tree\n200\t\n201\t- `stagedContent(repo, tree, relpath)`: if magic -> absent. Else\n202\t  `git -C <repo> cat-file blob <oid>:<relpath>`; non-zero exit -> absent;\n203\t  else the whole stdout.\n204\t- `stagedContentCeiling(repo, tree, relpath, ceiling)`: same guard and\n205\t  command, but streamed. Read up to `ceiling` bytes, then try one more\n206\t  byte. If that byte exists, `truncated = true` and the child is killed.\n207\t  If the process fails and the output was not truncated -> absent.\n208\t  Otherwise return the first `min(size, ceiling)` bytes plus `truncated`.\n209\t\n210\t### 2.5 `numberedSnippet(repo, relpath, maxBytes, tree)` -> string\n211\t\n212\tRenders one file with real 1-indexed line numbers, middle-truncated so\n213\tboth ends stay visible. Never throws; problems render as a notice. Below,\n214\t`EM` is U+2014 (em dash); these strings contain it literally in output.\n215\t\n216\tValidation:\n217\t\n218\t- Pinned and `!stagedPathCanonical(relpath)`, or live and\n219\t  `resolveLive` rejects:\n220\t  `### <relpath>\\n(path is outside the repository or invalid)\\n`.\n221\t\n222\tReading, with `readCeiling = 8 * maxBytes`:\n223\t\n224\t- Pinned: `stagedContentCeiling(..., readCeiling)`.\n225\t  - Absent, staged tree:\n226\t    `### <relpath>\\n(not in the index EM staged deletion?)\\n`\n227\t    (spaces around `EM`).\n228\t  - Absent, worktree tree:\n229\t    `### <relpath>\\n(not in the captured worktree)\\n`.\n230\t  - Truncated: header is `### <relpath> (<corpus> exceeds <readCeiling>\n231\t    bytes; only the first <readCeiling> were read EM true tail NOT shown\n232\t    below)` on one line, `<corpus>` = `staged blob` or `captured worktree\n233\t    blob`.\n234\t- Live: `stat(repo/relpath)` (follows symlinks). Error or not a regular\n235\t  file: `### <relpath>\\n(deleted or not a regular file)\\n`. If size >\n236\t  `readCeiling`, read only the first `readCeiling` bytes and set header\n237\t  `### <relpath> (file is <size> bytes; only the first <readCeiling> were\n238\t  read EM true tail NOT shown below)`. Read errors:\n239\t  `### <relpath>\\n(read error: <err>)\\n`.\n240\t\n241\tRendering. `lines` = content split on `\\n`, with one trailing empty\n242\telement dropped (empty content -> no lines). `\\r` is kept. A numbered line\n243\tis the line number right-aligned to width 5, two spaces, the text\n244\t(`%5d  %s`).\n245\t\n246\t- If `byteLength(content) <= maxBytes`: ``### <relpath>\\n```\\n`` + each\n247\t  numbered line + `\\n` + ```` ```\\n ````.\n248\t- Otherwise middle-truncate:\n249\t  1. `headBudget = trunc(maxBytes * 0.66)`. Walk lines from the top; a\n250\t     line costs `byteLength(line) + 1`; stop before the first line whose\n251\t     cost would push the total above `headBudget`. `headLast` is the last\n252\t     head line number (0 if none).\n253\t  2. `tailBudget = maxBytes - headBudget`. Walk 0-based indices `i` from\n254\t     the last line down while `i > headLast` (note: compares a 0-based\n255\t     index with a 1-based line number, so the line right after the head\n256\t     is never in the tail; keep this), stopping before exceeding\n257\t     `tailBudget` with the same cost rule. Reverse into ascending order.\n258\t     `tailFirst` = first tail line number, or `lines.length + 1` if none.\n259\t  3. If no header was set while reading: `### <relpath> (<n> lines,\n260\t     <bytes> bytes EM middle truncated)` with `<n>` = `lines.length` and\n261\t     `<bytes>` = byte length of the (possibly ceiling-cut) content.\n262\t  4. Output lines: header, ```` ``` ````, head numbered lines, then, if\n263\t     `tailFirst > headLast + 1`, the marker\n264\t     `       ... (<tailFirst - headLast - 1> lines omitted; real line\n265\t     numbers continue below)` (7 leading spaces, one line), tail numbered\n266\t     lines, ```` ``` ````, `''`; joined with `\\n` (so it ends in\n267\t     ```` ```\\n ````).\n268\t\n269\t### 2.6 `buildFileContext(repo, files, budget, tree)` -> string\n270\t\n271\t- `files` empty -> `(no files)`.\n272\t- For each file in order: `snippet = numberedSnippet(repo, f, 120000,\n273\t  tree)`. If `used + byteLength(snippet) > budget`, emit\n274\t  `### <f>\\n(omitted EM context budget exceeded)\\n` instead (it does not\n275\t  count against `used`) and continue with the next file (a later smaller\n276\t  file can still fit). Else emit the snippet and add its length to `used`.\n277\t- Join chunks with `\\n`.\n278\t\n279\tThe finder's user prompt (prompts area) embeds the post-fit diff and this\n280\tfile context for diff scopes, built from the scope's file list. For the\n281\t`repo` scope (survey mode) the finder gets the scope manifest instead and\n282\tno file context. There is no separate file-tree listing; the manifest is\n283\tthe scope area's.\n284\t\n285\t### 2.7 Exploration tools: common contract\n286\t\n287\t`explorationTools(repo, tree)` returns, in this order, `read_file`,\n288\t`list_files`, `grep_repo`. Who gets them (CLI and verify areas): the\n289\tfinder only in interactive mode; the verifier always.\n290\t\n291\t- Each tool's `run(args)` receives the decoded JSON arguments object and\n292\t  always resolves to a plain result object. Every failure the model can\n293\t  cause or hit (bad path, bad pattern, missing file, git failure) is a\n294\t  result `{\"error\": \"<message>\"}`, never a thrown error. Nothing in a tool\n295\t  is fatal to the review. (A thrown error or timeout is turned into\n296\t  `{\"error\": ...}` by the agent loop anyway.)\n297\t- Results are JSON-encoded by the llm area's single tool-result encoder\n298\t  (`spec.md` cross-cutting \"JSON encoding\"): object keys sorted, no HTML\n299\t  escaping of `<>&` or U+2028/U+2029. This area only builds plain result\n300\t  objects (keys listed alphabetically below for readability; the encoder\n301\t  owns the order). Go encodes a nil string slice as `null`; where that\n302\t  happens it is called out.\n303\t- `%q` below means Go `strconv.Quote` formatting (double quotes; `\\\"`,\n304\t  `\\\\`, `\\a\\b\\f\\n\\r\\t\\v`, other C0 controls and DEL as `\\xNN`, invalid\n305\t  UTF-8 bytes as `\\xNN`, printable Unicode kept as is). `'%s'` means the\n306\t  raw string inside single quotes.\n307\t\n308\tTool descriptions and schemas are sent to the model verbatim. In the\n309\tstrings below `\\u2014` is an em dash in the actual text.\n310\t\n311\tCorpus sentences, chosen by tree:\n312\t\n313\t```ts\n314\tconst CORPUS = {\n315\t    live: {\n316\t        read: 'Reads the working tree of this checkout (tracked plus ' +\n317\t            'untracked, non-ignored files; never .git).',\n318\t        grep: 'Search the working tree of this checkout (tracked plus ' +\n319\t            'untracked, non-ignored files) for a pattern.',\n320\t        list: 'Lists tracked plus untracked, non-ignored files in the ' +\n321\t            'working tree of this checkout.',\n322\t    },\n323\t    staged: {\n324\t        read: 'This review covers STAGED content, so this reads the git ' +\n325\t            'index blob \\u2014 exactly what the commit would contain ' +\n326\t            '\\u2014 NOT the working tree: unstaged edits and untracked ' +\n327\t            'files are invisible here, and a path staged for deletion ' +\n328\t            'reads as absent from the index.',\n329\t        grep: 'Search the git INDEX of this checkout for a pattern ' +\n330\t            '\\u2014 exactly the content the review covers. Unstaged ' +\n331\t            'edits and untracked files are NOT searched.',\n332\t        list: 'Lists regular files in the captured git INDEX, not the ' +\n333\t            'live working tree.',\n334\t    },\n335\t    worktree: {  // pinned, not staged\n336\t        read: 'Reads the immutable captured worktree used by this run, ' +\n337\t            'not later checkout edits.',\n338\t        grep: 'Search the immutable captured worktree used by this run, ' +\n339\t            'including captured non-ignored untracked files.',\n340\t        list: 'Lists regular files in the immutable captured worktree ' +\n341\t            'used by this run.',\n342\t    },\n343\t}\n344\t```\n345\t\n346\tTool definitions (schemas shown as JSON; Go emits object keys sorted):\n347\t\n348\t```ts\n349\tread_file: {\n350\t    description: 'Read a file from the repository under review. ' +\n351\t        corpus.read + ' Use when a finding depends on code you haven\\'t ' +\n352\t        'seen \\u2014 a helper, a type definition, a config, a test. ' +\n353\t        'For large files request a window via offset/limit.',\n354\t    schema: {\n355\t        type: 'object',\n356\t        properties: {\n357\t            path: {type: 'string', description: 'Repo-relative path.'},\n358\t            offset: {type: 'integer',\n359\t                description: '1-indexed starting line.'},\n360\t            limit: {type: 'integer', description: 'Max lines to return ' +\n361\t                '(default 500, at most 2000; output is capped at 65536 ' +\n362\t                'bytes).'},\n363\t        },\n364\t        required: ['path'],\n365\t    },\n366\t}\n367\tlist_files: {\n368\t    description: 'List files under a repo directory, optionally ' +\n369\t        'filtered by glob (e.g. \\'*.go\\', \\'**/*_test.go\\'). Returns ' +\n370\t        'relative paths. ' + corpus.list,\n371\t    schema: {\n372\t        type: 'object',\n373\t        properties: {\n374\t            directory: {type: 'string',\n375\t                description: 'Repo-relative directory (default: root).'},\n376\t            pattern: {type: 'string', description: 'Optional glob.'},\n377\t        },\n378\t    },\n379\t}\n380\tgrep_repo: {\n381\t    description: corpus.grep + ' Returns matching lines with filenames ' +\n382\t        'and line numbers. An empty result is not proof of absence ' +\n383\t        '\\u2014 it cannot see ignored/generated files, other repos, ' +\n384\t        'interface/reflection dispatch, or build-tag-excluded code.',\n385\t    schema: {\n386\t        type: 'object',\n387\t        properties: {\n388\t            pattern: {type: 'string', description: 'Search pattern ' +\n389\t                '(literal string or Go regexp).'},\n390\t            path_glob: {type: 'string', description: 'Optional glob to ' +\n391\t                'restrict which files are searched (e.g. \\'*.go\\').'},\n392\t        },\n393\t        required: ['pattern'],\n394\t    },\n395\t}\n396\t```\n397\t\n398\t#### `parseOffsetLimit(args)`\n399\t\n400\tDefaults `offset = 1`, `limit = 500`. For each of `offset`, `limit` that\n401\tis present and not `null`:\n402\t\n403\t- JSON number: truncate toward zero.\n404\t- String: parse like Go `fmt.Sscan` into an int: skip leading whitespace\n405\t  (including newlines), optional sign, base prefixes `0x`/`0o`/`0b` and\n406\t  `_` separators accepted, digits read as far as they go, trailing junk\n407\t  ignored (`\"12abc\"` -> 12, `\"1.5\"` -> 1). No digits -> error.\n408\t- Any other type (boolean, object, array) -> error.\n409\t- Values below 1 become 1.\n410\t- Finally each is clamped to at most `MAX_WINDOW` (2^30). This includes\n411\t  JSON numbers and numeric strings far beyond 2^63 or `Infinity`-like\n412\t  magnitudes: they clamp rather than error (Go's behavior there is\n413\t  platform-defined).\n414\t\n415\tAny error -> the tool returns `{\"error\": \"offset and limit must be\n416\tintegers\"}`.\n417\t\n418\t### 2.8 `read_file`\n419\t\n420\tArguments: `path` (string; a non-string counts as `''`), `offset`,\n421\t`limit`.\n422\t\n423\t1. Path check. Pinned: `!stagedPathCanonical(path)`. Live:\n424\t   `resolveLive(repo, path)` rejects. Either ->\n425\t   `error: path '<path>' is outside the repository or invalid`.\n426\t2. Existence, against the corpus being served, before the window is\n427\t   parsed:\n428\t   - Pinned: `git -C <repo> cat-file -e <oid>:<path>`. Non-zero exit ->\n429\t     staged tree: `<path> is not in the index EM staged deletion? this\n430\t     review covers the index, not the working tree` (one line; spaces\n431\t     around `EM`); worktree tree: `<path> is not in the captured worktree\n432\t     for this run`.\n433\t   - Live: `stat` of the resolved path (follows symlinks); error or not a\n434\t     regular file -> `<path> does not exist or is not a regular file`.\n435\t3. `parseOffsetLimit`; `limit = min(limit, 2000)`;\n436\t   `end = offset + limit` (first line not in the window).\n437\t4. Source. Pinned: spawn `git -C <repo> cat-file blob <oid>:<path>` and\n438\t   stream stdout; spawn failure -> `read failed: <err>`. Live: open the\n439\t   resolved path; failure -> `read failed: <err>`.\n440\t5. Line loop (identical for both corpora). Read one line at a time with\n441\t   `readLineCapped(MAX_LINE_BYTES)`: the line is consumed through its\n442\t   `\\n`, at most 32768 content bytes are kept, `cut` reports discarded\n443\t   bytes. Memory stays bounded by the read buffer, even for a\n444\t   multi-megabyte single-line blob, and a cut line never swallows the\n445\t   following line. For each line read (stop when a read returns no bytes\n446\t   at EOF; a final line without `\\n` still counts):\n447\t   1. `lineNo++`; strip the trailing `\\n`.\n448\t   2. If `cut`: push `lineNo` onto `truncatedLines` and append\n449\t      `  ... (line truncated at 32768 bytes)` to the text. This happens\n450\t      for every line read, including lines before `offset` and the line\n451\t      at `end`.\n452\t   3. If `lineNo >= end`: `moreBelow = true`; stop.\n453\t   4. If `lineNo >= offset`: `numbered = %5d  %s`; prefixed with `\\n` if\n454\t      the body is non-empty. If `body + numbered` would exceed 65536\n455\t      bytes: `end = lineNo`, `moreBelow = true`, `outputCapped = true`,\n456\t      stop. Else append. (32768 leaves room for at least one line.)\n457\t   5. Stop at EOF.\n458\t6. Pinned: if `moreBelow`, kill the `cat-file` process; then reap it\n459\t   (its exit status is ignored).\n460\t7. Trailer, first matching case only:\n461\t   - `outputCapped`: `\\n... (output capped at 65536 bytes; more lines\n462\t     below; request offset>=<end> to continue)`\n463\t   - `moreBelow`: `\\n... (more lines below; request offset>=<end> to\n464\t     continue)`\n465\t   - `lineNo === 0`: `\\n(file is empty)`\n466\t   - `offset > lineNo`: `\\n(offset <offset> is past EOF EM file has\n467\t     <lineNo> lines)`\n468\t\n469\t   Then, if `truncatedLines` is non-empty: `\\n(lines truncated at 32768\n470\t   bytes: [<n1> <n2> ...])` (Go `%v` of an int slice: space-separated in\n471\t   brackets).\n472\t8. Result:\n473\t\n474\t```json\n475\t{\n476\t  \"content\": \"<body + trailers>\",\n477\t  \"more_below\": true,\n478\t  \"path\": \"<path as given>\",\n479\t  \"returned_range\": [<offset>, <min(end - 1, lineNo)>]\n480\t}\n481\t```\n482\t\n483\t`returned_range` is a two-element integer array. Past EOF it is\n484\t`[offset, lineNo]` with `offset > lineNo`; for an empty file `[offset,\n485\t0]`. Example: a file whose first line is 100000 bytes, requested with\n486\t`offset 1, limit 3`, returns `[1, 1]` and a trailer naming `offset>=2`.\n487\t\n488\t### 2.9 `list_files`\n489\t\n490\tArguments: `directory` (string, default `.` when absent or empty),\n491\t`pattern` (string, default `*`).\n492\t\n493\t1. `byteLength(pattern) > 256` -> `pattern exceeds 256 bytes`.\n494\t2. Pinned: the directory must be `.` or (`stagedPathCanonical` and not\n495\t   ending in `/`); else `directory '<dir>' is outside the repository or\n496\t   invalid`. Live: see below.\n497\t3. Matching a repo-relative path `rel` (slash-separated, relative to the\n498\t   repo root, not to `directory`): `globstarMatch(pattern, rel) ||\n499\t   (!pattern.includes('/') && globstarMatch(pattern, basename(rel)))`.\n500\t4. Hidden: any segment of `rel` starting with `.` is never listed.\n501\t5. Accumulation (`listMatches.add`) in iteration order:\n502\t   - if 500 entries are already held: append\n503\t     `... (truncated at 500 matches)` and stop;\n504\t   - else if `bytes + byteLength(rel) + 1 > 65536`: append\n505\t     `... (truncated at 65536 bytes after <count> matches)` and stop;\n506\t   - else append `rel`, `bytes += byteLength(rel) + 1`.\n507\t6. Result `{\"directory\": <as given or \".\">, \"matches\": [...],\n508\t   \"pattern\": <as given or \"*\">}`. With zero matches Go emits\n509\t   `\"matches\": null`; keep that.\n510\t\n511\tLive tree:\n512\t\n513\t1. `resolveLive(repo, directory)` rejects -> `directory '<dir>' is\n514\t   outside the repository or invalid`. `stat` fails or not a directory ->\n515\t   `<dir> does not exist or is not a directory`. Relative-path error ->\n516\t   the \"outside\" message.\n517\t2. `relDir` = the resolved directory relative to realpath(`repo`).\n518\t   Literal git: `ls-files -z --cached --others --exclude-standard`, plus\n519\t   `-- <relDir>` unless `relDir === '.'`. Failure -> `list working tree\n520\t   failed: <err>` (`<err>` is the exec error, e.g. `exit status 128`, no\n521\t   stderr).\n522\t3. `snapshot.denylistedUntracked(repo)`; failure -> `list working tree\n523\t   failed: <err>`.\n524\t4. For each NUL-separated entry: skip empty, hidden, already seen\n525\t   (unmerged paths appear once per stage), denylisted-untracked, not\n526\t   matching, or whose `lstat(root/rel)` fails or is not a regular file\n527\t   (symlinks, submodules, index entries deleted on disk).\n528\t5. Sort byte-wise ascending, then accumulate.\n529\t\n530\tPinned tree:\n531\t\n532\t1. `git -C <repo> ls-tree -r -z --full-tree <oid>`; failure -> `list\n533\t   captured tree failed: <err>`.\n534\t2. Each NUL-separated record is `<mode> <type> <object>\\t<path>`. Keep\n535\t   only mode `100644` or `100755` with type `blob`.\n536\t3. If `directory` is not `.`, keep only paths starting with\n537\t   `<cleaned directory>/`.\n538\t4. Skip hidden, filter by match, accumulate in `ls-tree` order (no extra\n539\t   sort).\n540\t\n541\t#### `globstarMatch(pattern, value)`\n542\t\n543\tSplit both on `/`. Dynamic programming over segments: a pattern segment\n544\t`**` matches zero or more value segments; any other segment matches\n545\texactly one value segment by Go `path.Match` rules: `*` is any run of\n546\tnon-`/` characters, `?` one non-`/` character (a code point), `[...]` a\n547\tclass with optional leading `^` negation and `a-z` ranges, `\\` escapes\n548\tthe next character. A malformed segment pattern never matches. Example:\n549\t`**/*_test.go` matches `root_test.go`, `one/one_test.go`,\n550\t`one/two/two_test.go`, not `one/two/code.go`.\n551\t\n552\t### 2.10 `grep_repo`\n553\t\n554\tArguments: `pattern` (string, required), `path_glob` (string, optional).\n555\t\n556\t1. `pattern === ''` (or not a string) -> `pattern is required`.\n557\t2. Validate the pattern with JS `new RegExp(pattern)`; a throw ->\n558\t   `invalid pattern: <message>`. The message text differs from Go's RE2\n559\t   text and that is accepted. A pattern that passes this check but that\n560\t   `git grep -E` (POSIX ERE) rejects reaches step 5 and is reported to the\n561\t   model as `grep failed: ...`; git's stderr is the model's feedback for\n562\t   any remaining error.\n563\t3. Command, `git -C <repo> grep -n --no-color`, then:\n564\t   - live only: `--untracked` (tracked plus untracked files; it still\n565\t     honors `.gitignore`, so ignored build output is not searched);\n566\t   - `-E -e <pattern>`;\n567\t   - pinned only: `<oid>` (tree-ish after the pattern, before `--`);\n568\t   - `--`;\n569\t   - `<path_glob>` if non-empty (a Git pathspec; it is not made literal);\n570\t   - live only: `snapshot.denylistedUntracked(repo)` (failure -> `grep\n571\t     failed: <err>`); if non-empty and no `path_glob`, add `.`; then one\n572\t     `:(exclude,literal)<p>` per denied path.\n573\t\n574\t   The environment is the inherited one plus a forced\n575\t   `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0` (deliberate change 10): a user-exported\n576\t   `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1` would turn the `:(exclude,literal)` entries\n577\t   into plain paths and let denylisted untracked files be searched. The\n578\t   forced value is set for live and pinned calls alike. Spawn failure ->\n579\t   `grep failed: <err>`.\n580\t4. Stream stdout. `prefix` = `<oid>:` when pinned, else `''`. For each\n581\t   line, read with `readLineCapped(byteLength(prefix) + 2048)`:\n582\t   1. Strip the trailing `\\n`, then strip `prefix` if present (so pinned\n583\t      matches read `path:line:text`, the same shape as live).\n584\t   2. If the read was cut or the line is longer than 2048 bytes: keep the\n585\t      first 2048 bytes and append ` ... (line truncated)`.\n586\t   3. Skip empty lines.\n587\t   4. If 200 matches are held: append `... (truncated at 200 matching\n588\t      lines)`, mark capped, stop.\n589\t   5. If `used + byteLength(l) + 1 > 65536`: append `... (truncated at\n590\t      65536 bytes after <count> matching lines)`, mark capped, stop.\n591\t   6. Append; `used += byteLength(l) + 1`.\n592\t5. If capped, kill git and ignore its exit status. Otherwise: exit code 1\n593\t   -> the zero-match result; another failure -> `grep failed: <exec\n594\t   err>: <trimmed stderr>` (`<exec err>` like `exit status 128`).\n595\t6. Exit 0 but no usable lines -> the zero-match result.\n596\t7. Otherwise `{\"matches\": [...], \"pattern\": <pattern>}`.\n597\t\n598\tZero-match result (only on the zero-match path):\n599\t\n600\t```json\n601\t{\n602\t  \"matches\": [],\n603\t  \"note\": \"<NOTE>\",\n604\t  \"pattern\": \"<pattern>\",\n605\t  \"searched\": \"<SEARCHED>\"\n606\t}\n607\t```\n608\t\n609\t`<SEARCHED>`:\n610\t\n611\t- live: `tracked + untracked (non-ignored) files in the working tree of\n612\t  this checkout`\n613\t- staged: `the git INDEX of this checkout EM the staged content this\n614\t  review covers, NOT the working tree`\n615\t- captured worktree: `the immutable captured worktree used by this run`\n616\t- then, if `path_glob` is non-empty: `, restricted to path_glob %q`.\n617\t\n618\t`<NOTE>`, with `<BLIND>` = `unstaged working-tree edits, untracked files,\n619\t` for a staged tree and empty otherwise:\n620\t\n621\t```ts\n622\t'No matches. This is NOT proof the symbol is absent or unused: this ' +\n623\t'search cannot see ' + BLIND + '.gitignore\\'d or generated files, ' +\n624\t'other repositories, calls made through an interface, embedding, ' +\n625\t'reflection, or a struct/func value, code behind build tags, or a ' +\n626\t'name spelled differently at the call site. Do not cite an empty grep ' +\n627\t'as grounding for \\'no callers\\' / \\'never invoked\\' / ' +\n628\t'\\'unreachable\\' / \\'dead code\\'. If a verdict or a severity downgrade ' +\n629\t'depends on such a claim, establish it positively or report ' +\n630\t'insufficient_context.'\n631\t```\n632\t\n633\t### 2.11 Quote grounding\n634\t\n635\t`newQuoteGrounder(repo, tree, diff)` returns `ground(file, quote)` ->\n636\tboolean. The CLI builds it with the scope's diff (a `Buffer`, exactly the\n637\tbytes git printed) before `scope.fit` truncates it (a quote from past the\n638\ttruncation point is still grounded). `review.validate` drops a finding\n639\twhose quote does not ground (`DropUngroundedCodeQuote`, review area).\n640\t\n641\tThe corpus is exactly what the finder could have seen for the cited file,\n642\tno wider and no narrower. Corpora are keyed by the cited path exactly as\n643\tgiven; no path normalization.\n644\t\n645\t`ground(file, quote)`:\n646\t\n647\t1. `nq = normalizeWS(quote)`. If `nq` is empty or contains NUL -> false.\n648\t2. File corpus match (below) -> true.\n649\t3. `diffCorpora[file]` contains `nq` -> true; else false.\n650\t\n651\tMatching is on bytes, as in Go. The quote arrives as a string from the\n652\tllm area (lone surrogates already U+FFFD) and is UTF-8 encoded; file and\n653\tdiff bytes keep invalid UTF-8 as is. The scope area must hand the diff over\n654\tas a `Buffer` so a diff containing invalid UTF-8 grounds exactly as in Go\n655\t(`spec.md` cross-cutting \"Bytes and text\").\n656\t\n657\t#### `normalizeWS`\n658\t\n659\tCollapse every whitespace run to one space and trim both ends, exactly\n660\tlike Go `strings.Join(strings.Fields(s), \" \")`. Whitespace is Go\n661\t`unicode.IsSpace`: ASCII `\\t \\n \\v \\f \\r` and space, U+0085, U+00A0,\n662\tU+1680, U+2000..U+200A, U+2028, U+2029, U+202F, U+205F, U+3000. Not JS\n663\t`\\s` (which adds U+FEFF and lacks U+0085). NUL is not whitespace. Invalid\n664\tUTF-8 bytes are kept as bytes, not treated as whitespace. A streaming form\n665\t(`WsNormalizer.write(chunk, final)`) carries an incomplete UTF-8 sequence\n666\tacross chunks and must produce byte-identical output to the whole-string\n667\tform for any chunking.\n668\t\n669\t#### File corpus\n670\t\n671\t`fileSource(repo, tree, file)`:\n672\t\n673\t- Pinned: magic -> not in corpus. `git -C <repo> cat-file -s\n674\t  <oid>:<file>`; failure or unparsable size -> not in corpus. The opener\n675\t  streams `git -C <repo> cat-file blob <oid>:<file>`; its cleanup kills\n676\t  and reaps the process.\n677\t- Live: `resolveLive` rejects -> not in corpus. `stat` error or not a\n678\t  regular file -> not in corpus. The opener opens the file.\n679\t\n680\tMatching, with a per-grounder cache keyed by `file`:\n681\t\n682\t1. Cached -> `cache[file].includes(nq)`.\n683\t2. Not in corpus or the opener fails -> cache `''`, return false.\n684\t3. Size > 8 MiB -> stream the content through the normalizer in 64 KiB\n685\t   reads, keeping only the last `byteLength(nq)` normalized bytes between\n686\t   reads, and return whether `nq` appears. Not cached (re-streamed per\n687\t   call), so a huge file stays groundable with bounded memory.\n688\t4. Otherwise read up to 8 MiB, normalize, cache, test. A read error\n689\t   returns false without caching.\n690\t\n691\t#### Diff corpora: `splitDiffByFile(diffText)`\n692\t\n693\t1. Empty text -> no corpora.\n694\t2. Split into sections: a section ends just after the `\\n` that precedes\n695\t   each `\\ndiff --git ` occurrence. Text before the first `diff --git` is\n696\t   its own section.\n697\t3. `sectionPaths(section)`: walk lines (split on `\\n`). Once a line starts\n698\t   with `@@`, the rest of the section is hunk content and is not parsed\n699\t   for headers (so a deleted line `-- a/other.go`, rendered `--- a/other.go`,\n700\t   cannot steal the key). Before that:\n701\t   - `--- ` + path, where path may be C-quoted (`\"a/f\\303\\266.go\"`,\n702\t     decoded with Go `strconv.Unquote` rules: the whole remainder must be\n703\t     one quoted string; `\\a\\b\\f\\n\\r\\t\\v\\\\\\\"`, octal `\\ooo`, `\\xNN`,\n704\t     `\\uNNNN` escapes; bytes decoded as UTF-8), then prefix `a/` cut ->\n705\t     push path.\n706\t   - else `+++ ` + path, prefix `b/` -> push unless equal to the last\n707\t     pushed path.\n708\t   - `/dev/null` and unparsable quoting yield nothing.\n709\t4. A section with no paths contributes nothing (fail closed).\n710\t5. `normalizedDiffSides(section)` builds two normalized streams, `old`\n711\t   and `new`, each with its own streaming normalizer and an `any` flag:\n712\t   - line starts with `@@`: for each side whose `any` is set, write\n713\t     `\\n\\x00\\n` through that side's normalizer (a sentinel; `any` is not\n714\t     reset); then `inHunk = true`.\n715\t   - line starts with `diff --git `: `inHunk = false`.\n716\t   - not in a hunk, or empty line: ignore.\n717\t   - first byte ` `: rest of line + `\\n` to both sides, both `any = true`.\n718\t   - `-`: to `old` only; `+`: to `new` only.\n719\t   - anything else (`\\ No newline at end of file`): ignored.\n720\t   - Finish both normalizers. Corpus = `old` if `new` is empty, `new` if\n721\t     `old` is empty, else `old + ' \\x00 ' + new`.\n722\t\n723\t   Keeping sides apart rejects hybrid quotes spliced from a `-`/`+`\n724\t   interleave, and admits an old-side multiline quote whose lines have\n725\t   new-side lines between them.\n726\t6. For each path of the section, add the corpus under that key (skip an\n727\t   empty corpus); a key that already has a corpus is joined as\n728\t   `prev + ' \\x00 ' + norm`. Both old and new paths are keys, so a quote of\n729\t   deleted code grounds under the file's name.\n730\t\n731\t#### Grounding guarantees (from the tests)\n732\t\n733\t- `.git/config`, ignored, and untracked denylisted files never ground,\n734\t  even with the exact text; untracked non-ignored files do.\n735\t- A staged tree grounds against the index blob only, never the worktree;\n736\t  it also grounds against the staged diff.\n737\t- `0:foo.go` or `./foo.go` never ground against `foo.go`'s content.\n738\t- A quote at the end of a 16 MiB file grounds; an absent one does not.\n739\t\n740\t### 2.12 Security hardening summary\n741\t\n742\tEvery item below is pinned by a Go test (section 5).\n743\t\n744\t1. One review, one tree: all readers take the same `Tree`; a pinned tree\n745\t   is an object ID, never a ref, so concurrent `git add` or checkout edits\n746\t   do not change what is served. Under `GIT_INDEX_FILE` (the gate), the\n747\t   pinned index is the hook's temporary index, not the live one.\n748\t2. Live-tree confinement: no `.git` (any case, via `..`, or via a symlink\n749\t   into it), no ignored files, no untracked denylisted files; `check-ignore`\n750\t   failure fails closed. `list_files` asks Git for the file list instead\n751\t   of walking the disk, so ignored files never surface even by name.\n752\t3. Pinned-tree spelling: only canonical index paths reach `cat-file`, so\n753\t   stage selectors, `:/` commit search, and `./` aliases cannot return\n754\t   content under a fabricated key.\n755\t4. Pathspec safety: model paths follow `--`; live path checks use literal\n756\t   pathspecs (or `./` for `check-ignore`); `grep_repo` forces\n757\t   `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0` so the denylist exclusions stay magic.\n758\t5. Bounded output and memory: every tool result is capped at 64 KiB plus a\n759\t   short trailer; long lines are cut without buffering the whole line;\n760\t   blob readers kill the child process instead of draining it.\n761\t6. Honest absence: a zero-match grep says which tree was searched and\n762\t   that an empty result is not proof of absence.\n763\t\n764\t## 3. TypeScript module design\n765\t\n766\tDirectory `src/context/`. All functions that run git or read files take\n767\tan `AbortSignal` (Go ignores cancellation here; TS kills every git child\n768\tprocess on abort, per `spec.md` \"Process lifetime\", so none outlives the\n769\treview or a timed-out tool call).\n770\t\n771\t`src/context/tree.ts`\n772\t\n773\t```ts\n774\texport type Tree = {readonly oid:string; readonly worktree:boolean}\n775\texport const LIVE_TREE:Tree\n776\texport function pinned (t:Tree):boolean\n777\texport function staged (t:Tree):boolean\n778\texport function pinnedWorktree (oid:string):Tree\n779\texport function pinIndexTree (repo:string, signal:AbortSignal):\n780\t    Promise<Tree>  // throws Error with the messages in 2.2\n781\t```\n782\t\n783\t`src/context/git.ts` -- process helpers shared by this area.\n784\t\n785\t```ts\n786\t// execFile('git', ['-C', repo, ...args]); env = process.env + extra\n787\texport function git (repo:string, args:string[], opts?:{\n788\t    env?:Record<string, string>; literal?:boolean; signal?:AbortSignal\n789\t}):Promise<{code:number; stdout:Buffer; stderr:string}>\n790\t// every spawned child is killed when opts.signal aborts\n791\t// spawn with streamed stdout; kill() is safe after exit\n792\texport function gitStream (repo:string, args:string[], opts?:{...}):{\n793\t    stdout:AsyncIterable<Buffer>; kill():void\n794\t    done:Promise<{code:number|null; stderr:string}>\n795\t}\n796\t// read up to limit bytes, probe one more, kill if more remains\n797\texport function gitCapped (repo:string, args:string[], limit:number,\n798\t    signal?:AbortSignal):Promise<{ok:boolean; out:Buffer;\n799\t    truncated:boolean}>\n800\t```\n801\t\n802\t`src/context/lines.ts` -- `LineReader` over a byte stream with\n803\t`readLineCapped(cap):Promise<{line:Buffer; cut:boolean; eof:boolean}>`\n804\t(consumes through `\\n`, keeps at most `cap` content bytes, never\n805\taccumulates more). Used by `read_file` and `grep_repo`.\n806\t\n807\t`src/context/paths.ts`\n808\t\n809\t```ts\n810\texport function resolveUnderRepo (repo:string, p:string):string|null\n811\texport function resolveLive (repo:string, p:string,\n812\t    signal?:AbortSignal):Promise<string|null>\n813\texport function stagedPathCanonical (p:string):boolean\n814\t```\n815\t\n816\t`src/context/snippet.ts`\n817\t\n818\t```ts\n819\texport function numberedSnippet (repo:string, relpath:string,\n820\t    maxBytes:number, tree:Tree):Promise<string>\n821\texport function buildFileContext (repo:string, files:string[],\n822\t    budget:number, tree:Tree):Promise<string>\n823\t```\n824\t\n825\t`src/context/tools.ts`\n826\t\n827\t```ts\n828\texport function explorationTools (repo:string, tree:Tree):Tool[]\n829\t```\n830\t\n831\t`Tool` is the LLM area's type: `{name:string; description:string;\n832\tschema:object; run(args:Record<string, unknown>,\n833\tsignal:AbortSignal):Promise<unknown>}`. Tool bodies live in\n834\t`read-file.ts`, `list-files.ts`, `grep.ts`; each returns a plain object.\n835\tKey order and JSON text are the llm area's encoder's job.\n836\t\n837\t`src/context/glob.ts` -- `globstarMatch(pattern, value)` and a Go\n838\t`path.Match` port (`goPathMatch`, returns false on a malformed pattern).\n839\t\n840\t`src/context/normalize.ts` -- `normalizeWS(buf:Buffer):Buffer` and\n841\t`class WsNormalizer {write(chunk:Buffer, final:boolean):Buffer}`,\n842\tbyte-level (git output stays `Buffer`; see section 2.11).\n843\t\n844\t`src/context/grounding.ts`\n845\t\n846\t```ts\n847\texport type Grounder = (file:string, quote:string) => Promise<boolean>\n848\texport function newQuoteGrounder (repo:string, tree:Tree,\n849\t    diff:Buffer):Grounder\n850\texport function splitDiffByFile (diff:Buffer):Map<string, Buffer>\n851\t```\n852\t\n853\tThe grounder is async, so `review.validate` (review area) awaits it.\n854\t\n855\tShared helper (likely `src/util/goquote.ts`, owned with other areas):\n856\t`goQuote(s:string):string` reproducing `strconv.Quote` for `%q`.\n857\t\n858\tDependencies:\n859\t\n860\t- `src/llm` -- the `Tool` type only.\n861\t- `src/scope` -- the diff as a `Buffer` (the grounder's input).\n862\t- `src/snapshot` -- `isDenylisted(path)` and\n863\t  `denylistedUntracked(repo)` (untracked, non-ignored paths from\n864\t  `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard -z` that match the default\n865\t  denylist, case-folded, by basename or full path, sorted).\n866\t- Callers: `src/cli` (tree choice, file context, grounder, tools),\n867\t  `src/verify` (tools, verifier snippet via the CLI's prompt builder),\n868\t  `src/review` (grounder), `src/snapshot` (reuses `pinIndexTree`'s OID).\n869\t\n870\t## 4. Deliberate changes\n871\t\n872\tApplied from `spec.md` \"Deliberate changes\" and \"Cross-cutting rules\":\n873\t\n874\t- Change 23: the `git_diff` tool and the grounder's range corpus are not\n875\t  ported (`explorationTools` returns three tools; `newQuoteGrounder` takes\n876\t  no range).\n877\t- Change 10: `grep_repo` forces `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0`.\n878\t- Change 24 is not exercised here (names come from `-z` lists in scope).\n879\t- `grep_repo` validates patterns with JS `new RegExp`; error text differs\n880\t  from Go and git reports any other error back to the model.\n881\t- `parseOffsetLimit` clamps huge values to 2^30.\n882\t- Git output is `Buffer`; decoding with U+FFFD only for model-facing\n883\t  text; the diff reaches the grounder as `Buffer`.\n884\t- Tool-result JSON is encoded by the llm area (sorted keys, no HTML\n885\t  escaping).\n886\t- Every git child process is killed on abort.\n887\t- Model-facing em dashes (tool descriptions, trailers, `searched` text)\n888\t  are kept, written as `\\u2014` escapes in TS source; this doc and code\n889\t  comments never contain the character.\n890\t\n891\tWindows-only concerns in the Go code (case-insensitive `.git`\n892\tsegments are still needed on macOS) stay. Debug tracing (`roast:context`)\n893\tmay log tool names, paths, and result sizes, never file content, match\n894\tlines, or quotes.\n895\t\n896\t## 5. Test plan\n897\t\n898\tSeams. Most tests here need a temporary Git repository and call the area's\n899\tfunctions directly (module seam): the tools' guarantees are about bytes\n900\tand bounds that are expensive to observe through a whole review. A\n901\thandful of CLI-seam tests (in-process `run`, fake inference server that\n902\tscripts tool calls and records the tool results it receives) prove the\n903\twiring: the finder in interactive mode and the verifier receive the three\n904\ttools, results reach the model, and a staged review serves the index.\n905\tAssert on result objects (`error` present or absent, `more_below`,\n906\t`returned_range`, match membership, sizes, truncation markers), not on\n907\twhole human-readable strings.\n908\t\n909\tPath confinement\n910\t\n911\t- Empty, absolute, `..`, nested `..`, and symlink-out paths are rejected;\n912\t  normal paths and `.` accepted (TestResolveUnderRepo_PathEscape).\n913\t- Live tree serves only the review corpus: `read_file`,\n914\t  `numberedSnippet`, grounder, `grep_repo`, `list_files` never expose\n915\t  `.git/config`, `.GIT/config`, `.Git/HEAD`, `gitlink/config`, `.env`,\n916\t  `build/out.go`, `credentials.json`, `./.git/config`,\n917\t  `a/../.git/config`; tracked, untracked non-ignored, and tracked\n918\t  denylist-shaped files are served; `list_files` refuses `.git`,\n919\t  `build`, `gitlink` as directories\n920\t  (TestLiveTreeServesOnlyTheReviewCorpus).\n921\t- What the live guard blocks is exactly what pinning leaves out\n922\t  (TestPinnedTreeLeavesOutWhatTheLiveGuardBlocks).\n923\t- Pinned reads reject `0:foo.go`, `1:foo.go`, `/foo.go`, `:foo.go`, `''`,\n924\t  `./foo.go`, `../foo.go`, `sub/../foo.go`, `sub//foo.go`, `foo.go/.`;\n925\t  the grounder does not ground them; `read_file` errors\n926\t  (TestStagedContentRejectsGitRevisionMagic).\n927\t\n928\tTree selection\n929\t\n930\t- Staged readers serve the index, not the worktree\n931\t  (TestReadFileTool_StagedServesIndexNotWorktree,\n932\t  TestNumberedSnippet_StagedVsWorktree,\n933\t  TestGrepRepoTool_StagedSearchesTheIndex).\n934\t- Staged deletion is named as absent from the index\n935\t  (TestReadFileTool_StagedDeletionIsNamedNotJustAbsent).\n936\t- Captured-worktree readers ignore later checkout edits\n937\t  (TestCapturedWorktreeReadersIgnoreLaterCheckoutEdits).\n938\t- Under `GIT_INDEX_FILE`, all staged sources agree on the hook's index\n939\t  (TestStagedContextSourcesAgreeUnderGitIndexFile).\n940\t- Concurrent index writes after pinning are invisible\n941\t  (TestStagedReadersSeeOneTreeUnderConcurrentIndexWrites).\n942\t- Tool descriptions name the tree they serve\n943\t  (TestExplorationToolDescriptionsDiscloseTheirTree; assert the three\n944\t  variants differ per tree, not their text).\n945\t\n946\tFile context\n947\t\n948\t- Middle truncation keeps both ends with real line numbers\n949\t  (TestNumberedSnippet_Truncation); the captured-worktree ceiling header\n950\t  names its corpus (TestNumberedSnippetCapturedWorktreeTruncationLabel).\n951\t- Budget fitting omits files that do not fit and continues\n952\t  (TestBuildFileContext_BudgetFitting); empty list -> `(no files)`\n953\t  (TestBuildFileContext_Empty).\n954\t\n955\tread_file\n956\t\n957\t- Windowing, `more_below`, past-EOF (TestReadFileTool_Windowing).\n958\t- String offsets/limits are coerced (TestReadFileTool_StringIntCoercion).\n959\t- Staged windowing and pathological lines behave like live\n960\t  (TestReadFileTool_StagedWindowingAndPathologicalLines).\n961\t- A cut line does not swallow the next line\n962\t  (TestLongLineDoesNotSwallowTheNextLine).\n963\t- Huge single-line and huge multi-line blobs are not buffered whole\n964\t  (TestReadFileToolDoesNotBufferAWholeSingleLineBlob,\n965\t  TestReadFileToolDoesNotBufferWholeBlob). TS: assert the result is\n966\t  bounded and, through an injectable spawn, that the child was killed\n967\t  rather than measuring heap.\n968\t\n969\tlist_files\n970\t\n971\t- Hidden directories are skipped (TestListFilesTool_HiddenDirSkip).\n972\t- The 500-entry cap adds a marker (TestListFilesTool_Cap).\n973\t- `**` matches every depth (TestListFilesGlobstarMatchesEveryDepth).\n974\t\n975\tgrep_repo\n976\t\n977\t- Literal match, invalid regexp error, no-match result\n978\t  (TestGrepRepoTool_LiteralMatch, TestGrepRepoTool_InvalidRegexp,\n979\t  TestGrepRepoTool_NoMatch).\n980\t- Live grep sees untracked files under review and still honors\n981\t  `.gitignore` (TestGrepRepoTool_SeesUntrackedFilesUnderReview,\n982\t  TestGrepRepoTool_StillHonorsGitignore).\n983\t- Zero matches carry `note` and `searched`, and `searched` differs per\n984\t  tree (TestGrepRepoTool_ZeroMatchesCarriesAbsenceCaveat,\n985\t  TestGrepRepoTool_ZeroMatchesNamesWhichTreeWasSearched).\n986\t- Pinned matches are not OID-prefixed\n987\t  (TestGrepRepoTool_StagedMatchesAreNotOIDPrefixed).\n988\t\n989\tBounds (hardening)\n990\t\n991\t- Every tool result stays within 65536 bytes plus 512 bytes of trailer\n992\t  for wide, tall, minified, and many-match files and for 400 long paths,\n993\t  on both live and pinned trees; `wide.txt` with `limit 3` returns\n994\t  `returned_range [1, 1]` and resumes at `offset>=2`; grep and list mark\n995\t  truncation (TestToolResultsAreBounded).\n996\t\n997\tGrounding\n998\t\n999\t- Exact and whitespace-variant quotes ground; deleted code grounds via\n1000\t  its own file's section; diff content does not ground another file;\n1001\t  hybrid `-`/`+` quotes do not ground; old-side multiline quotes across an\n1002\t  interleave do; confabulated, empty, and NUL-carrying quotes do not;\n1003\t  header-shaped hunk lines do not steal the key; git-quoted paths key\n1004\t  their section (TestQuoteGrounder).\n1005\t- Staged corpus is the index only, plus the staged diff\n1006\t  (TestQuoteGrounderStagedCorpusIsTheIndex,\n1007\t  TestQuoteGrounderStagedAdmitsTheDiff).\n1008\t- A diff containing invalid UTF-8 bytes grounds a quote with the same\n1009\t  bytes and does not ground a quote with U+FFFD in their place (the diff\n1010\t  is a `Buffer`; new, no Go twin).\n1011\t- The normalizer equals the `strings.Fields` definition, including\n1012\t  non-ASCII whitespace and invalid UTF-8, and the streaming form equals\n1013\t  the whole form under random chunking (TestNormalizeWSMatchesFields).\n1014\t  Port as a property test with a reference implementation.\n1015\t- `normalizedDiffSides` equals a straightforward reference\n1016\t  (TestNormalizedDiffSidesMatchesReference); property test.\n1017\t- Huge files ground at the end without holding the file; a 1M-line diff\n1018\t  grounds (TestGrounderBoundsHugeFiles). TS: drop the allocation\n1019\t  assertions, keep the correctness ones.\n1020\t\n1021\tAdded for deliberate changes and cross-cutting rules (no Go twin)\n1022\t\n1023\t- `grep_repo` with `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1` exported in the parent\n1024\t  environment still excludes untracked denylisted files on a live tree\n1025\t  (spawn through an injectable seam and assert the child environment has\n1026\t  `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0`, plus a behavior check against a real repo).\n1027\t- Invalid patterns (`(`, `[a`) return `invalid pattern`; a pattern valid\n1028\t  for `new RegExp` but rejected by `git grep -E` returns `grep failed`\n1029\t  with git's stderr; assert on the `error` key, not its text.\n1030\t- `offset` and `limit` of `1e30`, `\"99999999999999999999\"`, and\n1031\t  `9007199254740993` clamp to 2^30 without an error; zero and negative\n1032\t  values become 1.\n1033\t- Aborting the signal kills the child of a streamed `read_file`,\n1034\t  `grep_repo`, and a blocking `cat-file -s`, observed through the\n1035\t  injectable spawn.\n1036\t- Tool results with `<`, `>`, `&`, U+2028 in file text reach the model\n1037\t  unescaped, with sorted keys (assert at the CLI seam on the recorded\n1038\t  request; the encoder itself is tested in the llm area).\n1039\t- `explorationTools` returns exactly `read_file`, `list_files`,\n1040\t  `grep_repo`.\n1041\t\n1042\t## 6. Go baseline references\n1043\t\n1044\tAt commit `cd08f10`:\n1045\t\n1046\t- `internal/context/context.go` -- constants, `ResolveUnderRepo`,\n1047\t  `resolveLive`, `liveCorpusAllows`, `StagedContent`,\n1048\t  `stagedContentCeiling`, `stagedPathIsMagic`, `NumberedSnippet`,\n1049\t  `BuildFileContext`, `ExplorationTools`, `GitDiffTool` (not ported), the\n1050\t  tool bodies, `readLineCapped`, `globstarMatch`, `noGrepMatches`,\n1051\t  `parseOffsetLimit`.\n1052\t- `internal/context/grounding.go` -- `NewQuoteGrounder`, `fileSource`,\n1053\t  `cappedOutput`, `streamContains`, `splitDiffByFile`, `sectionPaths`,\n1054\t  `cutDiffPath`, `normalizedDiffSides`, `normalizeWS`, `wsNormalizer`.\n1055\t- `internal/context/tree.go` -- `Tree`, `PinnedWorktree`,\n1056\t  `PinIndexTree`.\n1057\t- Tests: `internal/context/context_test.go`, `gitdiff_test.go`,\n1058\t  `grounding_test.go`, `hardening_test.go`, `list_files_test.go`.\n1059\t- Callers consulted: `internal/cli/cli.go` (tree choice, budgets\n1060\t  400000/100000, snippet sizes 120000/60000, grounder built pre-fit with\n1061\t  empty range, tool wiring), `internal/llm/agent.go` (results JSON-encoded,\n1062\t  errors become `{\"error\": ...}`), `internal/review/review.go`\n1063\t  (`Validate` drop order), `internal/scope/scope.go` (`PatchFlags`),\n1064\t  `internal/snapshot/snapshot.go` (denylist).\n1065\t\n1066\t## 7. Resolved\n1067\t\n1068\t1. `git_diff` and the grounder's range corpus are not ported; they are\n1069\t   absent on purpose (`spec.md` deliberate change 23).\n1070\t2. `grep_repo` patterns are validated with JS `new RegExp`; different\n1071\t   error text is accepted and git reports any remaining error to the model\n1072\t   (maintainer decision, section 2.10).\n1073\t3. Git output is `Buffer`; decode with U+FFFD only for model-facing text,\n1074\t   so scope hands the diff over as a `Buffer` (`spec.md` cross-cutting\n1075\t   \"Bytes and text\").\n1076\t4. `grep_repo` forces `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0` in the child environment\n1077\t   (`spec.md` deliberate change 10), with a test.\n1078\t5. Huge `offset` and `limit` numbers are clamped to 2^30 (`MAX_WINDOW`).\n1079\t6. Tool-result JSON is encoded by the llm area's encoder: sorted keys, no\n1080\t   HTML escaping (`spec.md` cross-cutting \"JSON encoding\").\n1081\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a2c0557b-d210-43b6-ab70-8720cf7cd94d","uuid":"09b306bb-e554-45a7-b6b2-e18143b510b5","timestamp":"2026-11-18T19:44:28.303Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-11-review-context/a2c0557b-d210-43b6-ab70-8720cf7cd94d/tool-results/b4583re9q.txt","content":"# 05 -- Review context, repository navigation tools, grounding\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nThis area decides what repository content the **finder** and **verifier**\ncan see during one review, and checks afterwards that every finding quotes\ncode the finder could actually have seen.\n\nIt has four parts:\n\n1. **The reviewed tree** (`Tree`). One review reads one corpus. A staged\n   review reads an immutable tree written from the index. A normal review\n   reads the immutable \"captured worktree\" tree (tracked files plus\n   untracked, non-ignored, non-denylisted files) that the snapshot area\n   writes. If no tree can be pinned, readers fall back to the live working\n   tree and enforce the same corpus rules on every read. Every reader in\n   this area (file context, the three exploration tools, the grounder)\n   takes the same `Tree`, so later checkout or index edits cannot change\n   what a running review sees.\n2. **File context** for the prompts: numbered, middle-truncated snippets\n   of the reviewed files for the finder's user prompt, and one snippet of\n   the cited file for each verifier prompt.\n3. **Exploration tools** handed to the agent loop: `read_file`,\n   `list_files`, `grep_repo` (finder in interactive mode, verifier\n   always). The Go baseline's `git_diff` tool and the grounder's range\n   corpus are dead code there and are intentionally absent from the TS\n   port (deliberate change 23 in `spec.md`).\n4. **Quote grounding**: the check `review.Validate` uses to drop a\n   finding whose `code_quote` does not appear (modulo whitespace) in the\n   cited file's content or the cited file's section of the reviewed diff.\n\nAll model-supplied paths and patterns are untrusted. Repository content is\nattacker-controlled. Every tool result is size-bounded and every path is\nconfined to the reviewed corpus; nothing in this area ever serves `.git`\ncontent, ignored files, or untracked denylisted credentials.\n\nThe agent loop that calls the tools, their per-call timeout, and the JSON\nencoding of results belong to the LLM area. The scope area owns the diff,\nthe file list, the manifest, `PatchFlags`, and fitting. The snapshot area\nowns `PinWorktree` and the secret denylist. The prompts area renders the\nuser prompts that embed this area's text.\n\n## 2. Behavior contract\n\n### 2.1 Constants and limits\n\nAll byte counts are UTF-8 bytes, never UTF-16 code units.\n\n| Name | Value | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `TOOL_OUTPUT_CAP` | 65536 | Ceiling on one tool result's content |\n| `MAX_LINE_BYTES` | 32768 | Ceiling on one served `read_file` line |\n| `READ_FILE_LINE_LIMIT` | 2000 | Max lines in one `read_file` window |\n| `GREP_LINE_BYTES` | 2048 | Ceiling on one `grep_repo` match line |\n| `LIST_FILES_CAP` | 500 | Max entries in one `list_files` result |\n| `GREP_MATCH_CAP` | 200 | Max match lines in one `grep_repo` result |\n| `GROUNDING_CACHE_BYTES` | 8388608 | Max file normalized whole |\n| `MAX_WINDOW` | 1073741824 | Clamp on parsed `offset` / `limit` (2^30) |\n| `LIST_PATTERN_MAX` | 256 | Max `list_files` pattern length in bytes |\n| default window | offset 1, limit 500 | `parseOffsetLimit` defaults |\n| stream buffer | 65536 | Read buffer for streamed git output |\n\nValues used by callers (CLI area) and restated here so the area is\ncomplete:\n\n- Finder file context: `BuildFileContext(repo, files, budget, tree)` with\n  `budget` = `limits.max_file_ctx_bytes` from the project config, default\n  400000, or 100000 under `--quick`. Each file's snippet is rendered with\n  `maxBytes` 120000.\n- Verifier snippet: `NumberedSnippet(repo, finding.file, 60000, tree)`.\n\n### 2.2 The reviewed tree\n\n```ts\ntype Tree = {\n    oid:string        // '' means the live working tree\n    worktree:boolean  // true: captured worktree; false + oid: index tree\n}\n```\n\n- `pinned(t)` is `t.oid !== ''`.\n- `staged(t)` is `pinned(t) && !t.worktree` (the index corpus).\n- `pinnedWorktree(oid)` returns `{oid, worktree: oid !== ''}`.\n- The zero tree `{oid: '', worktree: false}` is the live working tree.\n\nThe CLI chooses the tree once per review:\n\n1. `--staged`: `pinIndexTree(repo)` before building scope. Its failure is\n   fatal; the CLI wraps the message as `pin staged tree: <err>`.\n2. Otherwise, after building scope, `snapshot.pinWorktree(repo)`. On\n   success the tree is `pinnedWorktree(pin.tree)`. On failure the CLI\n   logs `could not pin the working tree (<err>); reading it live with\n   .git, ignored, and denylisted paths blocked` and uses the live tree.\n\n#### `pinIndexTree(repo)`\n\nWrites the current index into an immutable tree object without touching\nthe live index, any ref, or any commit.\n\n1. `git -C <repo> rev-parse --absolute-git-dir` -> `gitDir` (stdout\n   trimmed). Failure -> error `resolve git dir: <err>`.\n2. The live index path is `$GIT_INDEX_FILE` when set (joined onto `repo`\n   when relative), else `<gitDir>/index`. Honoring `GIT_INDEX_FILE` is\n   required: inside the pre-commit gate Git points it at a temporary index\n   holding exactly the commit's content.\n3. Create a temp directory with prefix `roast-pin-` under the OS temp dir;\n   failure -> `temp dir: <err>`. It is removed on every exit path.\n4. Copy the live index to `<tmp>/index` with mode `0600`. A missing live\n   index is not an error (Git treats it as an empty index): skip the copy.\n   Errors: `read <src>: <err>`, `create <dst>: <err>`, `copy index: <err>`.\n5. `git -C <repo> write-tree` with `GIT_INDEX_FILE=<tmp>/index` added to\n   the inherited environment. Failure -> `write-tree: <err>`. An index with\n   unmerged entries fails here.\n6. Return `{oid: <trimmed stdout>, worktree: false}`.\n\nGit failures in this helper fold stderr into the error:\n`<exec error>: <trimmed stderr>` when stderr is non-empty.\n\n### 2.3 Path confinement\n\n#### `resolveUnderRepo(repo, userPath)` -> absolute path or reject\n\nThe single chokepoint for model-supplied paths on the live tree.\n\n1. Reject `''` and absolute paths.\n2. `root` = realpath of `repo` (fall back to `repo` on error).\n3. `candidate` = `root` joined with the Go-`Clean`ed `userPath` (lexical:\n   collapse `.`/`..`/duplicate slashes, drop a trailing slash).\n4. `resolved` = `resolveDeepest(candidate)`: realpath of `candidate`; if\n   that fails, realpath of its parent, and so on up to `/`. The\n   non-existent tail is dropped, not re-appended.\n5. Reject unless `resolved === root` or `resolved` starts with\n   `root + '/'`.\n6. Reject if `inGitDir(root, candidate)` or `inGitDir(root, resolved)`:\n   the path relative to `root` has any segment equal to `.git`\n   case-insensitively (so `.GIT/config` is refused on case-insensitive\n   file systems). A relative-path computation error also rejects.\n7. Return `candidate` (not `resolved`).\n\nExamples: `''`, `/etc/passwd`, `../escape`, `sub/../../etc/passwd`, and\n`escape_link/secret.txt` (symlink to a directory outside the repo) are\nrejected; `internal/foo.go` and `.` are accepted.\n\n#### `resolveLive(repo, userPath)`\n\n`resolveUnderRepo`, then the corpus rules a pinned tree enforces by\nconstruction, applied to the path as given and, when different, to its\nsymlink target (`resolveDeepest(candidate)`), each taken relative to\n`root`:\n\n- `rel === '.'` is allowed.\n- If `rel` matches the secret denylist (snapshot area, `isDenylisted`) and\n  is not tracked, reject. Tracked means\n  `git -C <repo> ls-files -z --cached -- <rel>` (with\n  `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1`) exits 0 with non-empty stdout.\n- `git -C <repo> check-ignore -q -- ./<rel>` with\n  `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0` in the environment (check-ignore refuses\n  literal mode; the `./` prefix stops a leading `:` from being read as\n  pathspec magic). Allowed only when it exits with code 1 (\"not ignored\").\n  Exit 0 (ignored) or any other failure rejects (fail closed).\n\nExample: `gitlink -> .git` symlink: `gitlink/config` is rejected because\nits target is inside `.git`.\n\n#### `stagedPathIsMagic(relpath)` / `stagedPathCanonical(relpath)`\n\nPinned-tree reads spell blobs as `<oid>:<relpath>`, so a model-supplied\npath could land in Git's revision grammar. `stagedPathIsMagic` returns\ntrue (reject) when any of:\n\n- `relpath === ''` or starts with `:`;\n- `relpath[1] === ':'` and `relpath[0]` is `0`..`3` (merge stage selector,\n  e.g. `0:foo.go`);\n- splitting on `/` yields any segment that is `''`, `.` or `..` (covers\n  `/foo.go`, `./foo.go`, `../foo.go`, `sub/../foo.go`, `sub//foo.go`,\n  `foo.go/.`, and a trailing slash).\n\n`stagedPathCanonical` is its negation. A pinned-tree reader never runs\n`resolveUnderRepo`; canonical spelling is its only path check, and the\npinned tree contains no `.git`, ignored, or denylisted entries.\n\n#### Git invocation rules\n\n- Every git call is `git -C <repo> ...` via `execFile` (no shell), with\n  the inherited environment plus any listed overrides.\n- \"literal git\" means the environment gains `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1`.\n- Model-supplied paths always follow `--`.\n- Commands whose output may be large are streamed and the child process is\n  killed (not drained) once the reader has what it needs.\n- Every git child process this area spawns is also killed when the\n  caller's `AbortSignal` fires (`spec.md` cross-cutting \"Process\n  lifetime\"), including blocking calls such as `cat-file -s`,\n  `check-ignore`, `ls-files`, and `write-tree`.\n- Git output is read as `Buffer`. Bytes are decoded to a string with\n  U+FFFD replacement only where the text goes to a model (tool results,\n  prompt snippets). Hashing and matching paths stay on bytes (`spec.md`\n  cross-cutting \"Bytes and text\").\n\n### 2.4 Blob reads from a pinned tree\n\n- `stagedContent(repo, tree, relpath)`: if magic -> absent. Else\n  `git -C <repo> cat-file blob <oid>:<relpath>`; non-zero exit -> absent;\n  else the whole stdout.\n- `stagedContentCeiling(repo, tree, relpath, ceiling)`: same guard and\n  command, but streamed. Read up to `ceiling` bytes, then try one more\n  byte. If that byte exists, `truncated = true` and the child is killed.\n  If the process fails and the output was not truncated -> absent.\n  Otherwise return the first `min(size, ceiling)` bytes plus `truncated`.\n\n### 2.5 `numberedSnippet(repo, relpath, maxBytes, tree)` -> string\n\nRenders one file with real 1-indexed line numbers, middle-truncated so\nboth ends stay visible. Never throws; problems render as a notice. Below,\n`EM` is U+2014 (em dash); these strings contain it literally in output.\n\nValidation:\n\n- Pinned and `!stagedPathCanonical(relpath)`, or live and\n  `resolveLive` rejects:\n  `### <relpath>\\n(path is outside the repository or invalid)\\n`.\n\nReading, with `readCeiling = 8 * maxBytes`:\n\n- Pinned: `stagedContentCeiling(..., readCeiling)`.\n  - Absent, staged tree:\n    `### <relpath>\\n(not in the index EM staged deletion?)\\n`\n    (spaces around `EM`).\n  - Absent, worktree tree:\n    `### <relpath>\\n(not in the captured worktree)\\n`.\n  - Truncated: header is `### <relpath> (<corpus> exceeds <readCeiling>\n    bytes; only the first <readCeiling> were read EM true tail NOT shown\n    below)` on one line, `<corpus>` = `staged blob` or `captured worktree\n    blob`.\n- Live: `stat(repo/relpath)` (follows symlinks). Error or not a regular\n  file: `### <relpath>\\n(deleted or not a regular file)\\n`. If size >\n  `readCeiling`, read only the first `readCeiling` bytes and set header\n  `### <relpath> (file is <size> bytes; only the first <readCeiling> were\n  read EM true tail NOT shown below)`. Read errors:\n  `### <relpath>\\n(read error: <err>)\\n`.\n\nRendering. `lines` = content split on `\\n`, with one trailing empty\nelement dropped (empty content -> no lines). `\\r` is kept. A numbered line\nis the line number right-aligned to width 5, two spaces, the text\n(`%5d  %s`).\n\n- If `byteLength(content) <= maxBytes`: ``### <relpath>\\n```\\n`` + each\n  numbered line + `\\n` + ```` ```\\n ````.\n- Otherwise middle-truncate:\n  1. `headBudget = trunc(maxBytes * 0.66)`. Walk lines from the top; a\n     line costs `byteLength(line) + 1`; stop before the first line whose\n     cost would push the total above `headBudget`. `headLast` is the last\n     head line number (0 if none).\n  2. `tailBudget = maxBytes - headBudget`. Walk 0-based indices `i` from\n     the last line down while `i > headLast` (note: compares a 0-based\n     index with a 1-based line number, so the line right after the head\n     is never in the tail; keep this), stopping before exceeding\n     `tailBudget` with the same cost rule. Reverse into ascending order.\n     `tailFirst` = first tail line number, or `lines.length + 1` if none.\n  3. If no header was set while reading: `### <relpath> (<n> lines,\n     <bytes> bytes EM middle truncated)` with `<n>` = `lines.length` and\n     `<bytes>` = byte length of the (possibly ceiling-cut) content.\n  4. Output lines: header, ```` ``` ````, head numbered lines, then, if\n     `tailFirst > headLast + 1`, the marker\n     `       ... (<tailFirst - headLast - 1> lines omitted; real line\n     numbers continue below)` (7 leading spaces, one line), tail numbered\n     lines, ```` ``` ````, `''`; joined with `\\n` (so it ends in\n     ```` ```\\n ````).\n\n### 2.6 `buildFileContext(repo, files, budget, tree)` -> string\n\n- `files` empty -> `(no files)`.\n- For each file in order: `snippet = numberedSnippet(repo, f, 120000,\n  tree)`. If `used + byteLength(snippet) > budget`, emit\n  `### <f>\\n(omitted EM context budget exceeded)\\n` instead (it does not\n  count against `used`) and continue with the next file (a later smaller\n  file can still fit). Else emit the snippet and add its length to `used`.\n- Join chunks with `\\n`.\n\nThe finder's user prompt (prompts area) embeds the post-fit diff and this\nfile context for diff scopes, built from the scope's file list. For the\n`repo` scope (survey mode) the finder gets the scope manifest instead and\nno file context. There is no separate file-tree listing; the manifest is\nthe scope area's.\n\n### 2.7 Exploration tools: common contract\n\n`explorationTools(repo, tree)` returns, in this order, `read_file`,\n`list_files`, `grep_repo`. Who gets them (CLI and verify areas): the\nfinder only in interactive mode; the verifier always.\n\n- Each tool's `run(args)` receives the decoded JSON arguments object and\n  always resolves to a plain result object. Every failure the model can\n  cause or hit (bad path, bad pattern, missing file, git failure) is a\n  result `{\"error\": \"<message>\"}`, never a thrown error. Nothing in a tool\n  is fatal to the review. (A thrown error or timeout is turned into\n  `{\"error\": ...}` by the agent loop anyway.)\n- Results are JSON-encoded by the llm area's single tool-result encoder\n  (`spec.md` cross-cutting \"JSON encoding\"): object keys sorted, no HTML\n  escaping of `<>&` or U+2028/U+2029. This area only builds plain result\n  objects (keys listed alphabetically below for readability; the encoder\n  owns the order). Go encodes a nil string slice as `null`; where that\n  happens it is called out.\n- `%q` below means Go `strconv.Quote` formatting (double quotes; `\\\"`,\n  `\\\\`, `\\a\\b\\f\\n\\r\\t\\v`, other C0 controls and DEL as `\\xNN`, invalid\n  UTF-8 bytes as `\\xNN`, printable Unicode kept as is). `'%s'` means the\n  raw string inside single quotes.\n\nTool descriptions and schemas are sent to the model verbatim. In the\nstrings below `\\u2014` is an em dash in the actual text.\n\nCorpus sentences, chosen by tree:\n\n```ts\nconst CORPUS = {\n    live: {\n        read: 'Reads the working tree of this checkout (tracked plus ' +\n            'untracked, non-ignored files; never .git).',\n        grep: 'Search the working tree of this checkout (tracked plus ' +\n            'untracked, non-ignored files) for a pattern.',\n        list: 'Lists tracked plus untracked, non-ignored files in the ' +\n            'working tree of this checkout.',\n    },\n    staged: {\n        read: 'This review covers STAGED content, so this reads the git ' +\n            'index blob \\u2014 exactly what the commit would contain ' +\n            '\\u2014 NOT the working tree: unstaged edits and untracked ' +\n            'files are invisible here, and a path staged for deletion ' +\n            'reads as absent from the index.',\n        grep: 'Search the git INDEX of this checkout for a pattern ' +\n            '\\u2014 exactly the content the review covers. Unstaged ' +\n            'edits and untracked files are NOT searched.',\n        list: 'Lists regular files in the captured git INDEX, not the ' +\n            'live working tree.',\n    },\n    worktree: {  // pinned, not staged\n        read: 'Reads the immutable captured worktree used by this run, ' +\n            'not later checkout edits.',\n        grep: 'Search the immutable captured worktree used by this run, ' +\n            'including captured non-ignored untracked files.',\n        list: 'Lists regular files in the immutable captured worktree ' +\n            'used by this run.',\n    },\n}\n```\n\nTool definitions (schemas shown as JSON; Go emits object keys sorted):\n\n```ts\nread_file: {\n    description: 'Read a file from the repository under review. ' +\n        corpus.read + ' Use when a finding depends on code you haven\\'t ' +\n        'seen \\u2014 a helper, a type definition, a config, a test. ' +\n        'For large files request a window via offset/limit.',\n    schema: {\n        type: 'object',\n        properties: {\n            path: {type: 'string', description: 'Repo-relative path.'},\n            offset: {type: 'integer',\n                description: '1-indexed starting line.'},\n            limit: {type: 'integer', description: 'Max lines to return ' +\n                '(default 500, at most 2000; output is capped at 65536 ' +\n                'bytes).'},\n        },\n        required: ['path'],\n    },\n}\nlist_files: {\n    description: 'List files under a repo directory, optionally ' +\n        'filtered by glob (e.g. \\'*.go\\', \\'**/*_test.go\\'). Returns ' +\n        'relative paths. ' + corpus.list,\n    schema: {\n        type: 'object',\n        properties: {\n            directory: {type: 'string',\n                description: 'Repo-relative directory (default: root).'},\n            pattern: {type: 'string', description: 'Optional glob.'},\n        },\n    },\n}\ngrep_repo: {\n    description: corpus.grep + ' Returns matching lines with filenames ' +\n        'and line numbers. An empty result is not proof of absence ' +\n        '\\u2014 it cannot see ignored/generated files, other repos, ' +\n        'interface/reflection dispatch, or build-tag-excluded code.',\n    schema: {\n        type: 'object',\n        properties: {\n            pattern: {type: 'string', description: 'Search pattern ' +\n                '(literal string or Go regexp).'},\n            path_glob: {type: 'string', description: 'Optional glob to ' +\n                'restrict which files are searched (e.g. \\'*.go\\').'},\n        },\n        required: ['pattern'],\n    },\n}\n```\n\n#### `parseOffsetLimit(args)`\n\nDefaults `offset = 1`, `limit = 500`. For each of `offset`, `limit` that\nis present and not `null`:\n\n- JSON number: truncate toward zero.\n- String: parse like Go `fmt.Sscan` into an int: skip leading whitespace\n  (including newlines), optional sign, base prefixes `0x`/`0o`/`0b` and\n  `_` separators accepted, digits read as far as they go, trailing junk\n  ignored (`\"12abc\"` -> 12, `\"1.5\"` -> 1). No digits -> error.\n- Any other type (boolean, object, array) -> error.\n- Values below 1 become 1.\n- Finally each is clamped to at most `MAX_WINDOW` (2^30). This includes\n  JSON numbers and numeric strings far beyond 2^63 or `Infinity`-like\n  magnitudes: they clamp rather than error (Go's behavior there is\n  platform-defined).\n\nAny error -> the tool returns `{\"error\": \"offset and limit must be\nintegers\"}`.\n\n### 2.8 `read_file`\n\nArguments: `path` (string; a non-string counts as `''`), `offset`,\n`limit`.\n\n1. Path check. Pinned: `!stagedPathCanonical(path)`. Live:\n   `resolveLive(repo, path)` rejects. Either ->\n   `error: path '<path>' is outside the repository or invalid`.\n2. Existence, against the corpus being served, before the window is\n   parsed:\n   - Pinned: `git -C <repo> cat-file -e <oid>:<path>`. Non-zero exit ->\n     staged tree: `<path> is not in the index EM staged deletion? this\n     review covers the index, not the working tree` (one line; spaces\n     around `EM`); worktree tree: `<path> is not in the captured worktree\n     for this run`.\n   - Live: `stat` of the resolved path (follows symlinks); error or not a\n     regular file -> `<path> does not exist or is not a regular file`.\n3. `parseOffsetLimit`; `limit = min(limit, 2000)`;\n   `end = offset + limit` (first line not in the window).\n4. Source. Pinned: spawn `git -C <repo> cat-file blob <oid>:<path>` and\n   stream stdout; spawn failure -> `read failed: <err>`. Live: open the\n   resolved path; failure -> `read failed: <err>`.\n5. Line loop (identical for both corpora). Read one line at a time with\n   `readLineCapped(MAX_LINE_BYTES)`: the line is consumed through its\n   `\\n`, at most 32768 content bytes are kept, `cut` reports discarded\n   bytes. Memory stays bounded by the read buffer, even for a\n   multi-megabyte single-line blob, and a cut line never swallows the\n   following line. For each line read (stop when a read returns no bytes\n   at EOF; a final line without `\\n` still counts):\n   1. `lineNo++`; strip the trailing `\\n`.\n   2. If `cut`: push `lineNo` onto `truncatedLines` and append\n      `  ... (line truncated at 32768 bytes)` to the text. This happens\n      for every line read, including lines before `offset` and the line\n      at `end`.\n   3. If `lineNo >= end`: `moreBelow = true`; stop.\n   4. If `lineNo >= offset`: `numbered = %5d  %s`; prefixed with `\\n` if\n      the body is non-empty. If `body + numbered` would exceed 65536\n      bytes: `end = lineNo`, `moreBelow = true`, `outputCapped = true`,\n      stop. Else append. (32768 leaves room for at least one line.)\n   5. Stop at EOF.\n6. Pinned: if `moreBelow`, kill the `cat-file` process; then reap it\n   (its exit status is ignored).\n7. Trailer, first matching case only:\n   - `outputCapped`: `\\n... (output capped at 65536 bytes; more lines\n     below; request offset>=<end> to continue)`\n   - `moreBelow`: `\\n... (more lines below; request offset>=<end> to\n     continue)`\n   - `lineNo === 0`: `\\n(file is empty)`\n   - `offset > lineNo`: `\\n(offset <offset> is past EOF EM file has\n     <lineNo> lines)`\n\n   Then, if `truncatedLines` is non-empty: `\\n(lines truncated at 32768\n   bytes: [<n1> <n2> ...])` (Go `%v` of an int slice: space-separated in\n   brackets).\n8. Result:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"content\": \"<body + trailers>\",\n  \"more_below\": true,\n  \"path\": \"<path as given>\",\n  \"returned_range\": [<offset>, <min(end - 1, lineNo)>]\n}\n```\n\n`returned_range` is a two-element integer array. Past EOF it is\n`[offset, lineNo]` with `offset > lineNo`; for an empty file `[offset,\n0]`. Example: a file whose first line is 100000 bytes, requested with\n`offset 1, limit 3`, returns `[1, 1]` and a trailer naming `offset>=2`.\n\n### 2.9 `list_files`\n\nArguments: `directory` (string, default `.` when absent or empty),\n`pattern` (string, default `*`).\n\n1. `byteLength(pattern) > 256` -> `pattern exceeds 256 bytes`.\n2. Pinned: the directory must be `.` or (`stagedPathCanonical` and not\n   ending in `/`); else `directory '<dir>' is outside the repository or\n   invalid`. Live: see below.\n3. Matching a repo-relative path `rel` (slash-separated, relative to the\n   repo root, not to `directory`): `globstarMatch(pattern, rel) ||\n   (!pattern.includes('/') && globstarMatch(pattern, basename(rel)))`.\n4. Hidden: any segment of `rel` starting with `.` is never listed.\n5. Accumulation (`listMatches.add`) in iteration order:\n   - if 500 entries are already held: append\n     `... (truncated at 500 matches)` and stop;\n   - else if `bytes + byteLength(rel) + 1 > 65536`: append\n     `... (truncated at 65536 bytes after <count> matches)` and stop;\n   - else append `rel`, `bytes += byteLength(rel) + 1`.\n6. Result `{\"directory\": <as given or \".\">, \"matches\": [...],\n   \"pattern\": <as given or \"*\">}`. With zero matches Go emits\n   `\"matches\": null`; keep that.\n\nLive tree:\n\n1. `resolveLive(repo, directory)` rejects -> `directory '<dir>' is\n   outside the repository or invalid`. `stat` fails or not a directory ->\n   `<dir> does not exist or is not a directory`. Relative-path error ->\n   the \"outside\" message.\n2. `relDir` = the resolved directory relative to realpath(`repo`).\n   Literal git: `ls-files -z --cached --others --exclude-standard`, plus\n   `-- <relDir>` unless `relDir === '.'`. Failure -> `list working tree\n   failed: <err>` (`<err>` is the exec error, e.g. `exit status 128`, no\n   stderr).\n3. `snapshot.denylistedUntracked(repo)`; failure -> `list working tree\n   failed: <err>`.\n4. For each NUL-separated entry: skip empty, hidden, already seen\n   (unmerged paths appear once per stage), denylisted-untracked, not\n   matching, or whose `lstat(root/rel)` fails or is not a regular file\n   (symlinks, submodules, index entries deleted on disk).\n5. Sort byte-wise ascending, then accumulate.\n\nPinned tree:\n\n1. `git -C <repo> ls-tree -r -z --full-tree <oid>`; failure -> `list\n   captured tree failed: <err>`.\n2. Each NUL-separated record is `<mode> <type> <object>\\t<path>`. Keep\n   only mode `100644` or `100755` with type `blob`.\n3. If `directory` is not `.`, keep only paths starting with\n   `<cleaned directory>/`.\n4. Skip hidden, filter by match, accumulate in `ls-tree` order (no extra\n   sort).\n\n#### `globstarMatch(pattern, value)`\n\nSplit both on `/`. Dynamic programming over segments: a pattern segment\n`**` matches zero or more value segments; any other segment matches\nexactly one value segment by Go `path.Match` rules: `*` is any run of\nnon-`/` characters, `?` one non-`/` character (a code point), `[...]` a\nclass with optional leading `^` negation and `a-z` ranges, `\\` escapes\nthe next character. A malformed segment pattern never matches. Example:\n`**/*_test.go` matches `root_test.go`, `one/one_test.go`,\n`one/two/two_test.go`, not `one/two/code.go`.\n\n### 2.10 `grep_repo`\n\nArguments: `pattern` (string, required), `path_glob` (string, optional).\n\n1. `pattern === ''` (or not a string) -> `pattern is required`.\n2. Validate the pattern with JS `new RegExp(pattern)`; a throw ->\n   `invalid pattern: <message>`. The message text differs from Go's RE2\n   text and that is accepted. A pattern that passes this check but that\n   `git grep -E` (POSIX ERE) rejects reaches step 5 and is reported to the\n   model as `grep failed: ...`; git's stderr is the model's feedback for\n   any remaining error.\n3. Command, `git -C <repo> grep -n --no-color`, then:\n   - live only: `--untracked` (tracked plus untracked files; it still\n     honors `.gitignore`, so ignored build output is not searched);\n   - `-E -e <pattern>`;\n   - pinned only: `<oid>` (tree-ish after the pattern, before `--`);\n   - `--`;\n   - `<path_glob>` if non-empty (a Git pathspec; it is not made literal);\n   - live only: `snapshot.denylistedUntracked(repo)` (failure -> `grep\n     failed: <err>`); if non-empty and no `path_glob`, add `.`; then one\n     `:(exclude,literal)<p>` per denied path.\n\n   The environment is the inherited one plus a forced\n   `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0` (deliberate change 10): a user-exported\n   `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1` would turn the `:(exclude,literal)` entries\n   into plain paths and let denylisted untracked files be searched. The\n   forced value is set for live and pinned calls alike. Spawn failure ->\n   `grep failed: <err>`.\n4. Stream stdout. `prefix` = `<oid>:` when pinned, else `''`. For each\n   line, read with `readLineCapped(byteLength(prefix) + 2048)`:\n   1. Strip the trailing `\\n`, then strip `prefix` if present (so pinned\n      matches read `path:line:text`, the same shape as live).\n   2. If the read was cut or the line is longer than 2048 bytes: keep the\n      first 2048 bytes and append ` ... (line truncated)`.\n   3. Skip empty lines.\n   4. If 200 matches are held: append `... (truncated at 200 matching\n      lines)`, mark capped, stop.\n   5. If `used + byteLength(l) + 1 > 65536`: append `... (truncated at\n      65536 bytes after <count> matching lines)`, mark capped, stop.\n   6. Append; `used += byteLength(l) + 1`.\n5. If capped, kill git and ignore its exit status. Otherwise: exit code 1\n   -> the zero-match result; another failure -> `grep failed: <exec\n   err>: <trimmed stderr>` (`<exec err>` like `exit status 128`).\n6. Exit 0 but no usable lines -> the zero-match result.\n7. Otherwise `{\"matches\": [...], \"pattern\": <pattern>}`.\n\nZero-match result (only on the zero-match path):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matches\": [],\n  \"note\": \"<NOTE>\",\n  \"pattern\": \"<pattern>\",\n  \"searched\": \"<SEARCHED>\"\n}\n```\n\n`<SEARCHED>`:\n\n- live: `tracked + untracked (non-ignored) files in the working tree of\n  this checkout`\n- staged: `the git INDEX of this checkout EM the staged content this\n  review covers, NOT the working tree`\n- captured worktree: `the immutable captured worktree used by this run`\n- then, if `path_glob` is non-empty: `, restricted to path_glob %q`.\n\n`<NOTE>`, with `<BLIND>` = `unstaged working-tree edits, untracked files,\n` for a staged tree and empty otherwise:\n\n```ts\n'No matches. This is NOT proof the symbol is absent or unused: this ' +\n'search cannot see ' + BLIND + '.gitignore\\'d or generated files, ' +\n'other repositories, calls made through an interface, embedding, ' +\n'reflection, or a struct/func value, code behind build tags, or a ' +\n'name spelled differently at the call site. Do not cite an empty grep ' +\n'as grounding for \\'no callers\\' / \\'never invoked\\' / ' +\n'\\'unreachable\\' / \\'dead code\\'. If a verdict or a severity downgrade ' +\n'depends on such a claim, establish it positively or report ' +\n'insufficient_context.'\n```\n\n### 2.11 Quote grounding\n\n`newQuoteGrounder(repo, tree, diff)` returns `ground(file, quote)` ->\nboolean. The CLI builds it with the scope's diff (a `Buffer`, exactly the\nbytes git printed) before `scope.fit` truncates it (a quote from past the\ntruncation point is still grounded). `review.validate` drops a finding\nwhose quote does not ground (`DropUngroundedCodeQuote`, review area).\n\nThe corpus is exactly what the finder could have seen for the cited file,\nno wider and no narrower. Corpora are keyed by the cited path exactly as\ngiven; no path normalization.\n\n`ground(file, quote)`:\n\n1. `nq = normalizeWS(quote)`. If `nq` is empty or contains NUL -> false.\n2. File corpus match (below) -> true.\n3. `diffCorpora[file]` contains `nq` -> true; else false.\n\nMatching is on bytes, as in Go. The quote arrives as a string from the\nllm area (lone surrogates already U+FFFD) and is UTF-8 encoded; file and\ndiff bytes keep invalid UTF-8 as is. The scope area must hand the diff over\nas a `Buffer` so a diff containing invalid UTF-8 grounds exactly as in Go\n(`spec.md` cross-cutting \"Bytes and text\").\n\n#### `normalizeWS`\n\nCollapse every whitespace run to one space and trim both ends, exactly\nlike Go `strings.Join(strings.Fields(s), \" \")`. Whitespace is Go\n`unicode.IsSpace`: ASCII `\\t \\n \\v \\f \\r` and space, U+0085, U+00A0,\nU+1680, U+2000..U+200A, U+2028, U+2029, U+202F, U+205F, U+3000. Not JS\n`\\s` (which adds U+FEFF and lacks U+0085). NUL is not whitespace. Invalid\nUTF-8 bytes are kept as bytes, not treated as whitespace. A streaming form\n(`WsNormalizer.write(chunk, final)`) carries an incomplete UTF-8 sequence\nacross chunks and must produce byte-identical output to the whole-string\nform for any chunking.\n\n#### File corpus\n\n`fileSource(repo, tree, file)`:\n\n- Pinned: magic -> not in corpus. `git -C <repo> cat-file -s\n  <oid>:<file>`; failure or unparsable size -> not in corpus. The opener\n  streams `git -C <repo> cat-file blob <oid>:<file>`; its cleanup kills\n  and reaps the process.\n- Live: `resolveLive` rejects -> not in corpus. `stat` error or not a\n  regular file -> not in corpus. The opener opens the file.\n\nMatching, with a per-grounder cache keyed by `file`:\n\n1. Cached -> `cache[file].includes(nq)`.\n2. Not in corpus or the opener fails -> cache `''`, return false.\n3. Size > 8 MiB -> stream the content through the normalizer in 64 KiB\n   reads, keeping only the last `byteLength(nq)` normalized bytes between\n   reads, and return whether `nq` appears. Not cached (re-streamed per\n   call), so a huge file stays groundable with bounded memory.\n4. Otherwise read up to 8 MiB, normalize, cache, test. A read error\n   returns false without caching.\n\n#### Diff corpora: `splitDiffByFile(diffText)`\n\n1. Empty text -> no corpora.\n2. Split into sections: a section ends just after the `\\n` that precedes\n   each `\\ndiff --git ` occurrence. Text before the first `diff --git` is\n   its own section.\n3. `sectionPaths(section)`: walk lines (split on `\\n`). Once a line starts\n   with `@@`, the rest of the section is hunk content and is not parsed\n   for headers (so a deleted line `-- a/other.go`, rendered `--- a/other.go`,\n   cannot steal the key). Before that:\n   - `--- ` + path, where path may be C-quoted (`\"a/f\\303\\266.go\"`,\n     decoded with Go `strconv.Unquote` rules: the whole remainder must be\n     one quoted string; `\\a\\b\\f\\n\\r\\t\\v\\\\\\\"`, octal `\\ooo`, `\\xNN`,\n     `\\uNNNN` escapes; bytes decoded as UTF-8), then prefix `a/` cut ->\n     push path.\n   - else `+++ ` + path, prefix `b/` -> push unless equal to the last\n     pushed path.\n   - `/dev/null` and unparsable quoting yield nothing.\n4. A section with no paths contributes nothing (fail closed).\n5. `normalizedDiffSides(section)` builds two normalized streams, `old`\n   and `new`, each with its own streaming normalizer and an `any` flag:\n   - line starts with `@@`: for each side whose `any` is set, write\n     `\\n\\x00\\n` through that side's normalizer (a sentinel; `any` is not\n     reset); then `inHunk = true`.\n   - line starts with `diff --git `: `inHunk = false`.\n   - not in a hunk, or empty line: ignore.\n   - first byte ` `: rest of line + `\\n` to both sides, both `any = true`.\n   - `-`: to `old` only; `+`: to `new` only.\n   - anything else (`\\ No newline at end of file`): ignored.\n   - Finish both normalizers. Corpus = `old` if `new` is empty, `new` if\n     `old` is empty, else `old + ' \\x00 ' + new`.\n\n   Keeping sides apart rejects hybrid quotes spliced from a `-`/`+`\n   interleave, and admits an old-side multiline quote whose lines have\n   new-side lines between them.\n6. For each path of the section, add the corpus under that key (skip an\n   empty corpus); a key that already has a corpus is joined as\n   `prev + ' \\x00 ' + norm`. Both old and new paths are keys, so a quote of\n   deleted code grounds under the file's name.\n\n#### Grounding guarantees (from the tests)\n\n- `.git/config`, ignored, and untracked denylisted files never ground,\n  even with the exact text; untracked non-ignored files do.\n- A staged tree grounds against the index blob only, never the worktree;\n  it also grounds against the staged diff.\n- `0:foo.go` or `./foo.go` never ground against `foo.go`'s content.\n- A quote at the end of a 16 MiB file grounds; an absent one does not.\n\n### 2.12 Security hardening summary\n\nEvery item below is pinned by a Go test (section 5).\n\n1. One review, one tree: all readers take the same `Tree`; a pinned tree\n   is an object ID, never a ref, so concurrent `git add` or checkout edits\n   do not change what is served. Under `GIT_INDEX_FILE` (the gate), the\n   pinned index is the hook's temporary index, not the live one.\n2. Live-tree confinement: no `.git` (any case, via `..`, or via a symlink\n   into it), no ignored files, no untracked denylisted files; `check-ignore`\n   failure fails closed. `list_files` asks Git for the file list instead\n   of walking the disk, so ignored files never surface even by name.\n3. Pinned-tree spelling: only canonical index paths reach `cat-file`, so\n   stage selectors, `:/` commit search, and `./` aliases cannot return\n   content under a fabricated key.\n4. Pathspec safety: model paths follow `--`; live path checks use literal\n   pathspecs (or `./` for `check-ignore`); `grep_repo` forces\n   `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0` so the denylist exclusions stay magic.\n5. Bounded output and memory: every tool result is capped at 64 KiB plus a\n   short trailer; long lines are cut without buffering the whole line;\n   blob readers kill the child process instead of draining it.\n6. Honest absence: a zero-match grep says which tree was searched and\n   that an empty result is not proof of absence.\n\n## 3. TypeScript module design\n\nDirectory `src/context/`. All functions that run git or read files take\nan `AbortSignal` (Go ignores cancellation here; TS kills every git child\nprocess on abort, per `spec.md` \"Process lifetime\", so none outlives the\nreview or a timed-out tool call).\n\n`src/context/tree.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport type Tree = {readonly oid:string; readonly worktree:boolean}\nexport const LIVE_TREE:Tree\nexport function pinned (t:Tree):boolean\nexport function staged (t:Tree):boolean\nexport function pinnedWorktree (oid:string):Tree\nexport function pinIndexTree (repo:string, signal:AbortSignal):\n    Promise<Tree>  // throws Error with the messages in 2.2\n```\n\n`src/context/git.ts` -- process helpers shared by this area.\n\n```ts\n// execFile('git', ['-C', repo, ...args]); env = process.env + extra\nexport function git (repo:string, args:string[], opts?:{\n    env?:Record<string, string>; literal?:boolean; signal?:AbortSignal\n}):Promise<{code:number; stdout:Buffer; stderr:string}>\n// every spawned child is killed when opts.signal aborts\n// spawn with streamed stdout; kill() is safe after exit\nexport function gitStream (repo:string, args:string[], opts?:{...}):{\n    stdout:AsyncIterable<Buffer>; kill():void\n    done:Promise<{code:number|null; stderr:string}>\n}\n// read up to limit bytes, probe one more, kill if more remains\nexport function gitCapped (repo:string, args:string[], limit:number,\n    signal?:AbortSignal):Promise<{ok:boolean; out:Buffer;\n    truncated:boolean}>\n```\n\n`src/context/lines.ts` -- `LineReader` over a byte stream with\n`readLineCapped(cap):Promise<{line:Buffer; cut:boolean; eof:boolean}>`\n(consumes through `\\n`, keeps at most `cap` content bytes, never\naccumulates more). Used by `read_file` and `grep_repo`.\n\n`src/context/paths.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport function resolveUnderRepo (repo:string, p:string):string|null\nexport function resolveLive (repo:string, p:string,\n    signal?:AbortSignal):Promise<string|null>\nexport function stagedPathCanonical (p:string):boolean\n```\n\n`src/context/snippet.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport function numberedSnippet (repo:string, relpath:string,\n    maxBytes:number, tree:Tree):Promise<string>\nexport function buildFileContext (repo:string, files:string[],\n    budget:number, tree:Tree):Promise<string>\n```\n\n`src/context/tools.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport function explorationTools (repo:string, tree:Tree):Tool[]\n```\n\n`Tool` is the LLM area's type: `{name:string; description:string;\nschema:object; run(args:Record<string, unknown>,\nsignal:AbortSignal):Promise<unknown>}`. Tool bodies live in\n`read-file.ts`, `list-files.ts`, `grep.ts`; each returns a plain object.\nKey order and JSON text are the llm area's encoder's job.\n\n`src/context/glob.ts` -- `globstarMatch(pattern, value)` and a Go\n`path.Match` port (`goPathMatch`, returns false on a malformed pattern).\n\n`src/context/normalize.ts` -- `normalizeWS(buf:Buffer):Buffer` and\n`class WsNormalizer {write(chunk:Buffer, final:boolean):Buffer}`,\nbyte-level (git output stays `Buffer`; see section 2.11).\n\n`src/context/grounding.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport type Grounder = (file:string, quote:string) => Promise<boolean>\nexport function newQuoteGrounder (repo:string, tree:Tree,\n    diff:Buffer):Grounder\nexport function splitDiffByFile (diff:Buffer):Map<string, Buffer>\n```\n\nThe grounder is async, so `review.validate` (review area) awaits it.\n\nShared helper (likely `src/util/goquote.ts`, owned with other areas):\n`goQuote(s:string):string` reproducing `strconv.Quote` for `%q`.\n\nDependencies:\n\n- `src/llm` -- the `Tool` type only.\n- `src/scope` -- the diff as a `Buffer` (the grounder's input).\n- `src/snapshot` -- `isDenylisted(path)` and\n  `denylistedUntracked(repo)` (untracked, non-ignored paths from\n  `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard -z` that match the default\n  denylist, case-folded, by basename or full path, sorted).\n- Callers: `src/cli` (tree choice, file context, grounder, tools),\n  `src/verify` (tools, verifier snippet via the CLI's prompt builder),\n  `src/review` (grounder), `src/snapshot` (reuses `pinIndexTree`'s OID).\n\n## 4. Deliberate changes\n\nApplied from `spec.md` \"Deliberate changes\" and \"Cross-cutting rules\":\n\n- Change 23: the `git_diff` tool and the grounder's range corpus are not\n  ported (`explorationTools` returns three tools; `newQuoteGrounder` takes\n  no range).\n- Change 10: `grep_repo` forces `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0`.\n- Change 24 is not exercised here (names come from `-z` lists in scope).\n- `grep_repo` validates patterns with JS `new RegExp`; error text differs\n  from Go and git reports any other error back to the model.\n- `parseOffsetLimit` clamps huge values to 2^30.\n- Git output is `Buffer`; decoding with U+FFFD only for model-facing\n  text; the diff reaches the grounder as `Buffer`.\n- Tool-result JSON is encoded by the llm area (sorted keys, no HTML\n  escaping).\n- Every git child process is killed on abort.\n- Model-facing em dashes (tool descriptions, trailers, `searched` text)\n  are kept, written as `\\u2014` escapes in TS source; this doc and code\n  comments never contain the character.\n\nWindows-only concerns in the Go code (case-insensitive `.git`\nsegments are still needed on macOS) stay. Debug tracing (`roast:context`)\nmay log tool names, paths, and result sizes, never file content, match\nlines, or quotes.\n\n## 5. Test plan\n\nSeams. Most tests here need a temporary Git repository and call the area's\nfunctions directly (module seam): the tools' guarantees are about bytes\nand bounds that are expensive to observe through a whole review. A\nhandful of CLI-seam tests (in-process `run`, fake inference server that\nscripts tool calls and records the tool results it receives) prove the\nwiring: the finder in interactive mode and the verifier receive the three\ntools, results reach the model, and a staged review serves the index.\nAssert on result objects (`error` present or absent, `more_below`,\n`returned_range`, match membership, sizes, truncation markers), not on\nwhole human-readable strings.\n\nPath confinement\n\n- Empty, absolute, `..`, nested `..`, and symlink-out paths are rejected;\n  normal paths and `.` accepted (TestResolveUnderRepo_PathEscape).\n- Live tree serves only the review corpus: `read_file`,\n  `numberedSnippet`, grounder, `grep_repo`, `list_files` never expose\n  `.git/config`, `.GIT/config`, `.Git/HEAD`, `gitlink/config`, `.env`,\n  `build/out.go`, `credentials.json`, `./.git/config`,\n  `a/../.git/config`; tracked, untracked non-ignored, and tracked\n  denylist-shaped files are served; `list_files` refuses `.git`,\n  `build`, `gitlink` as directories\n  (TestLiveTreeServesOnlyTheReviewCorpus).\n- What the live guard blocks is exactly what pinning leaves out\n  (TestPinnedTreeLeavesOutWhatTheLiveGuardBlocks).\n- Pinned reads reject `0:foo.go`, `1:foo.go`, `/foo.go`, `:foo.go`, `''`,\n  `./foo.go`, `../foo.go`, `sub/../foo.go`, `sub//foo.go`, `foo.go/.`;\n  the grounder does not ground them; `read_file` errors\n  (TestStagedContentRejectsGitRevisionMagic).\n\nTree selection\n\n- Staged readers serve the index, not the worktree\n  (TestReadFileTool_StagedServesIndexNotWorktree,\n  TestNumberedSnippet_StagedVsWorktree,\n  TestGrepRepoTool_StagedSearchesTheIndex).\n- Staged deletion is named as absent from the index\n  (TestReadFileTool_StagedDeletionIsNamedNotJustAbsent).\n- Captured-worktree readers ignore later checkout edits\n  (TestCapturedWorktreeReadersIgnoreLaterCheckoutEdits).\n- Under `GIT_INDEX_FILE`, all staged sources agree on the hook's index\n  (TestStagedContextSourcesAgreeUnderGitIndexFile).\n- Concurrent index writes after pinning are invisible\n  (TestStagedReadersSeeOneTreeUnderConcurrentIndexWrites).\n- Tool descriptions name the tree they serve\n  (TestExplorationToolDescriptionsDiscloseTheirTree; assert the three\n  variants differ per tree, not their text).\n\nFile context\n\n- Middle truncation keeps both ends with real line numbers\n  (TestNumberedSnippet_Truncation); the captured-worktree ceiling header\n  names its corpus (TestNumberedSnippetCapturedWorktreeTruncationLabel).\n- Budget fitting omits files that do not fit and continues\n  (TestBuildFileContext_BudgetFitting); empty list -> `(no files)`\n  (TestBuildFileContext_Empty).\n\nread_file\n\n- Windowing, `more_below`, past-EOF (TestReadFileTool_Windowing).\n- String offsets/limits are coerced (TestReadFileTool_StringIntCoercion).\n- Staged windowing and pathological lines behave like live\n  (TestReadFileTool_StagedWindowingAndPathologicalLines).\n- A cut line does not swallow the next line\n  (TestLongLineDoesNotSwallowTheNextLine).\n- Huge single-line and huge multi-line blobs are not buffered whole\n  (TestReadFileToolDoesNotBufferAWholeSingleLineBlob,\n  TestReadFileToolDoesNotBufferWholeBlob). TS: assert the result is\n  bounded and, through an injectable spawn, that the child was killed\n  rather than measuring heap.\n\nlist_files\n\n- Hidden directories are skipped (TestListFilesTool_HiddenDirSkip).\n- The 500-entry cap adds a marker (TestListFilesTool_Cap).\n- `**` matches every depth (TestListFilesGlobstarMatchesEveryDepth).\n\ngrep_repo\n\n- Literal match, invalid regexp error, no-match result\n  (TestGrepRepoTool_LiteralMatch, TestGrepRepoTool_InvalidRegexp,\n  TestGrepRepoTool_NoMatch).\n- Live grep sees untracked files under review and still honors\n  `.gitignore` (TestGrepRepoTool_SeesUntrackedFilesUnderReview,\n  TestGrepRepoTool_StillHonorsGitignore).\n- Zero matches carry `note` and `searched`, and `searched` differs per\n  tree (TestGrepRepoTool_ZeroMatchesCarriesAbsenceCaveat,\n  TestGrepRepoTool_ZeroMatchesNamesWhichTreeWasSearched).\n- Pinned matches are not OID-prefixed\n  (TestGrepRepoTool_StagedMatchesAreNotOIDPrefixed).\n\nBounds (hardening)\n\n- Every tool result stays within 65536 bytes plus 512 bytes of trailer\n  for wide, tall, minified, and many-match files and for 400 long paths,\n  on both live and pinned trees; `wide.txt` with `limit 3` returns\n  `returned_range [1, 1]` and resumes at `offset>=2`; grep and list mark\n  truncation (TestToolResultsAreBounded).\n\nGrounding\n\n- Exact and whitespace-variant quotes ground; deleted code grounds via\n  its own file's section; diff content does not ground another file;\n  hybrid `-`/`+` quotes do not ground; old-side multiline quotes across an\n  interleave do; confabulated, empty, and NUL-carrying quotes do not;\n  header-shaped hunk lines do not steal the key; git-quoted paths key\n  their section (TestQuoteGrounder).\n- Staged corpus is the index only, plus the staged diff\n  (TestQuoteGrounderStagedCorpusIsTheIndex,\n  TestQuoteGrounderStagedAdmitsTheDiff).\n- A diff containing invalid UTF-8 bytes grounds a quote with the same\n  bytes and does not ground a quote with U+FFFD in their place (the diff\n  is a `Buffer`; new, no Go twin).\n- The normalizer equals the `strings.Fields` definition, including\n  non-ASCII whitespace and invalid UTF-8, and the streaming form equals\n  the whole form under random chunking (TestNormalizeWSMatchesFields).\n  Port as a property test with a reference implementation.\n- `normalizedDiffSides` equals a straightforward reference\n  (TestNormalizedDiffSidesMatchesReference); property test.\n- Huge files ground at the end without holding the file; a 1M-line diff\n  grounds (TestGrounderBoundsHugeFiles). TS: drop the allocation\n  assertions, keep the correctness ones.\n\nAdded for deliberate changes and cross-cutting rules (no Go twin)\n\n- `grep_repo` with `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=1` exported in the parent\n  environment still excludes untracked denylisted files on a live tree\n  (spawn through an injectable seam and assert the child environment has\n  `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0`, plus a behavior check against a real repo).\n- Invalid patterns (`(`, `[a`) return `invalid pattern`; a pattern valid\n  for `new RegExp` but rejected by `git grep -E` returns `grep failed`\n  with git's stderr; assert on the `error` key, not its text.\n- `offset` and `limit` of `1e30`, `\"99999999999999999999\"`, and\n  `9007199254740993` clamp to 2^30 without an error; zero and negative\n  values become 1.\n- Aborting the signal kills the child of a streamed `read_file`,\n  `grep_repo`, and a blocking `cat-file -s`, observed through the\n  injectable spawn.\n- Tool results with `<`, `>`, `&`, U+2028 in file text reach the model\n  unescaped, with sorted keys (assert at the CLI seam on the recorded\n  request; the encoder itself is tested in the llm area).\n- `explorationTools` returns exactly `read_file`, `list_files`,\n  `grep_repo`.\n\n## 6. Go baseline references\n\nAt commit `cd08f10`:\n\n- `internal/context/context.go` -- constants, `ResolveUnderRepo`,\n  `resolveLive`, `liveCorpusAllows`, `StagedContent`,\n  `stagedContentCeiling`, `stagedPathIsMagic`, `NumberedSnippet`,\n  `BuildFileContext`, `ExplorationTools`, `GitDiffTool` (not ported), the\n  tool bodies, `readLineCapped`, `globstarMatch`, `noGrepMatches`,\n  `parseOffsetLimit`.\n- `internal/context/grounding.go` -- `NewQuoteGrounder`, `fileSource`,\n  `cappedOutput`, `streamContains`, `splitDiffByFile`, `sectionPaths`,\n  `cutDiffPath`, `normalizedDiffSides`, `normalizeWS`, `wsNormalizer`.\n- `internal/context/tree.go` -- `Tree`, `PinnedWorktree`,\n  `PinIndexTree`.\n- Tests: `internal/context/context_test.go`, `gitdiff_test.go`,\n  `grounding_test.go`, `hardening_test.go`, `list_files_test.go`.\n- Callers consulted: `internal/cli/cli.go` (tree choice, budgets\n  400000/100000, snippet sizes 120000/60000, grounder built pre-fit with\n  empty range, tool wiring), `internal/llm/agent.go` (results JSON-encoded,\n  errors become `{\"error\": ...}`), `internal/review/review.go`\n  (`Validate` drop order), `internal/scope/scope.go` (`PatchFlags`),\n  `internal/snapshot/snapshot.go` (denylist).\n\n## 7. Resolved\n\n1. `git_diff` and the grounder's range corpus are not ported; they are\n   absent on purpose (`spec.md` deliberate change 23).\n2. `grep_repo` patterns are validated with JS `new RegExp`; different\n   error text is accepted and git reports any remaining error to the model\n   (maintainer decision, section 2.10).\n3. Git output is `Buffer`; decode with U+FFFD only for model-facing text,\n   so scope hands the diff over as a `Buffer` (`spec.md` cross-cutting\n   \"Bytes and text\").\n4. `grep_repo` forces `GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS=0` in the child environment\n   (`spec.md` deliberate change 10), with a test.\n5. Huge `offset` and `limit` numbers are clamped to 2^30 (`MAX_WINDOW`).\n6. Tool-result JSON is encoded by the llm area's encoder: sorted keys, no\n   HTML escaping (`spec.md` cross-cutting \"JSON encoding\").\n","numLines":1081,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1081}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018ix8cSZArxU8LgGwm2z2hn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
